\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 The Digest\b0\par\par\uc2 By John Koblin and The Associated Press \par \uc2 540 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 February 2025\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 ENTERTAINMENT\par \par \uc2 Audience Drops 9%\par \par \uc2 For Grammy Awards\par \par \uc2 Viewership for the Grammys slipped to 15.4 million on Sunday night, according to Nielsen, a 9 percent decline compared with last year's ceremony. The drop snaps a three-year streak for the Grammys. Ratings steadily increased during that period, culminating with nearly 17 million viewers for last year's show. Ratings had started to climb after 2021, when a stripped-down pandemic ceremony saw its audience fall to roughly nine million viewers.\par \par \uc2 Even with the decline this year, the Grammys, which aired on \uc2 CBS\uc2  and streamed on Paramount+, is well positioned to be one of the most-watched entertainment shows of 2025. And its ratings among adults under the age of 50 declined only modestly.\par \par \uc2 Grammys producers had cautioned before the event that they did not expect a ratings bump this year. Given the devastation of the wildfires, the Grammys delayed its annual marketing blitz in the run-up to the event.\par \par \uc2 Still, the ratings decline could be a warning sign for the coming Academy Awards, which will air on ABC and stream on \uc2 Hulu\uc2  on March 2. Ratings for awards shows have generally been on the upswing since 2022, and the Oscars is no exception: It has had three consecutive years of ratings growth, leading to last year's audience of 19.5 million viewers.\par \par \uc2 The Golden Globes ratings slipped to 9.3 million viewers last month, nearly a million viewers shy of last year. The Oscars commanded $80 million in advertising revenue for ABC last year, while the Grammys brought in $47 million, according to Guideline, an ad data firm. JOHN KOBLIN\par \par \uc2 RETAIL\par \par \uc2 Est\u233\'00\'E9e Lauder\uc2  to Cut\par \par \uc2 Up to 7,000 Jobs\par \par \uc2 Est\u233\'00\'E9e Lauder\uc2  may trim as many as 7,000 jobs by 2026, more than 11% of its workforce, after the global beauty cosmetics maker lost money in its most recent quarter and reported a 6% sales slump.\par \par \uc2 The New York company behind such brands as MAC, La Mer and Aveda tempered its profit outlook as the economies of China and Korea slow, in addition to global geopolitical uncertainty.\par \par \uc2 China announced retaliatory tariffs on some American imports and an antitrust investigation into \uc2 Google\uc2  on Tuesday, just minutes after a sweeping levy on Chinese products imposed by President Trump took effect.\par \par \uc2 Est\u233\'00\'E9e Lauder\uc2  expects to book restructuring and other charges related to the job cuts of between $1.2 billion and $1.6 billion, before taxes.\par \par \uc2 As of June 30, 2024, \uc2 Est\u233\'00\'E9e Lauder\uc2  had roughly 62,000 employees worldwide, according to the company's latest annual filing.\par \par \uc2 ''We are significantly transforming our operating model to be leaner, faster and more agile,'' said \uc2 St\u233\'00\'E9phane de La Faverie\uc2 , who became the company's chief executive last month.\par \par \uc2 The company posted sales of $4 billion in the latest quarter, down from $4.28 billion in the same period last year. It now expects profit per share between 24 cents and 34 cents in the current quarter, far below the 61 cents per share that Wall Street had been expecting, according to \uc2 FactSet\uc2 . Shares of \uc2 The Est\u233\'00\'E9e Lauder Companies Inc.\uc2  fell nearly 15%, or $12.14, Tuesday. ASSOCIATED PRESS\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020250205el250004j\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
