\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Trump Warns the E.P.A. of Potential Firings\b0\par\par\uc2 By Lisa Friedman \par \uc2 889 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 February 2025\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 21\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 A spokeswoman for \uc2 Lee Zeldin\uc2 , the new head of the agency, said the goal was to create an ''effective and efficient'' federal work force.\par \par \uc2 The Trump administration has warned more than 1,100 \uc2 Environmental Protection Agency\uc2  employees who work on climate change, reducing air pollution, enforcing environmental laws and other programs that they could be fired at any time.\par \par \uc2 An email, reviewed by \uc2 The New York Times\uc2 , was sent to staff members who were hired within the past year and have probationary status. Many of those employees were encouraged to join the E.P.A. under the Biden administration to rebuild the agency, which had been depleted during President Trump's first term. Others are experienced federal workers who had taken new assignments within the agency.\par \par \uc2 Many had been hired to work on programs that Congress created through two recent laws, doing things like helping communities replace lead pipes, remediating toxic sites and funding clean energy projects aimed at reducing the greenhouse gas emissions that are heating the planet.\par \par \uc2 ''As a probationary/trial period employee, the agency has the right to immediately terminate you,'' the email states.\par \par \uc2 Molly Vaseliou, an E.P.A. spokeswoman, said in a statement that ''our goal is to be transparent.'' She declined to answer questions about the email, though, including whether \uc2 Lee Zeldin\uc2 , the agency's new administrator, intended to terminate employees and, if so, for what reason.\par \par \uc2 ''On his first day in office, he engaged directly with career staff across E.P.A.'s headquarters -- spanning two city blocks in downtown D.C. -- listening to their insights and perspectives,'' Ms. Vaseliou said. ''Ultimately, the goal is to create a more effective and efficient federal government that serves all Americans.''\par \par \uc2 At 9:21 a.m. on Monday, E.P.A. employees received another email notifying them of that the agencywide intranet was out of service. Without the internal agency network, employees cannot access documents or other information needed for their jobs.\par \par \uc2 The email from E.P.A.'s Office of Mission Support reads ''Access to work.epa.gov is current unavailable'' and that technical specialists were working to resolve the issue. It was not immediately clear if the outage was related to efforts to reduce the work force.\par \par \uc2 Asked about the email, Ms. Vaseliou said ''There was an outage.''\par \par \uc2 Other federal agencies have been directed by the Office of Personnel Management to submit lists of probationary employees, but E.P.A. workers appear to be the first to receive notice that they may be immediately dismissed.\par \par \uc2 Leaders at the \uc2 American Federation of Government Employees\uc2 , which represents unionized E.P.A. employees, called the move a clear attempt to gut an agency that Mr. Trump dislikes. Through the E.P.A., the Biden administration developed aggressive regulations to curb planet-warming pollution from power plants, automobiles and oil and gas wells.\par \par \uc2 ''E.P.A. is at the center of the bullseye for President Trump's vindictive purge of public servants,'' said Michelle Roos, executive director of the Environmental Protection Network, a group of agency alumni.\par \par \uc2 She called it ''the most chaotic and vindictive transition in the history of the \uc2 Environmental Protection Agency\uc2 .''\par \par \uc2 Two E.P.A. employees who received the email said it had caused them to rethink the Trump administration's offer to federal employees to resign but be paid through the end of September. The employees, who spoke on the condition of anonymity because they are concerned about being fired, said they had initially dismissed that offer as untrustworthy.\par \par \uc2 Being informed that their jobs are particularly precarious has caused them to rethink their options, both said.\par \par \uc2 Probationary employees are considered easier to fire because they do not have the full range of civil service protections, but rules still exist, said Marie Owens-Powell, president of the federation of government employees union, which represents about 8,000 E.P.A. workers.\par \par \uc2 ''There has to be cause, and the cause can't be because you're a Democrat and it's a Republican administration,'' Ms. Owens-Powell said. The union is consulting lawyers about whether the administration can fire workers based on a simple declaration of a change in agency priorities, she said.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Owens-Powell said the Trump administration had placed about 15 E.P.A. employees who were working on diversity, equity and inclusion efforts on administrative leave. She said the next likely targets are those working on environmental justice, or programs to help marginalized communities that suffer from disproportionate amounts of air and water pollution.\par \par \uc2 Staffing at the E.P.A. peaked in 2004 during the \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2  administration, when there were 17,611 employees, according to the agency. Those levels ebbed and flowed slightly, but began to take a sharp dip during the Obama administration amid Republican control of the House and Senate.\par \par \uc2 When Mr. Trump entered the White House in 2017, the E.P.A. had 15,408 employees. The following year that number dropped to 14,172 as political appointees reversed regulations, shut scientists out of decision-making and shrank the agency's budget.\par \par \uc2 The Biden administration sought to reverse those losses. The agency currently employs about 15,130 people around the country, a level slightly higher than when \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2  was president.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020250204el240003k\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
