\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  and Union Agree To Mediation for Contract\b0\par\par\uc2 By Danielle Kaye and Rebecca Davis O'Brien \par \uc2 653 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 February 2025\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 6\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 A labor agreement, covering more than 500 U.S. stores, would be the company's first. Talks stalled last year over wages.\par \par \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  and the union representing thousands of its employees said on Thursday that they were bringing in a mediator in a push to revive contract talks, which had stalled over an impasse on wage increases.\par \par \uc2 In a joint statement, the two sides said that they had made progress over the last nine months, and that they were ''committed to continuing to work together -- with a mediator's assistance -- to navigate complex issues and reach fair contracts.''\par \par \uc2 Workers United, the union that represents \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  workers at more than 500 company-owned stores across the United States, had hoped to finalize a framework by the end of 2024 that would form the basis of contracts at individual unionized stores. But \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  did not offer a substantial wage increase during the latest bargaining session in December, the union said. At the time, the company called the union's wage proposals ''not sustainable.''\par \par \uc2 The standoff prompted a five-day strike that began in about 10 to 15 stores in Los Angeles, Seattle and Chicago and spread to dozens more in major cities during the busy holiday season, union representatives said at the time.\par \par \uc2 The union effort at \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  began in Buffalo in 2021, and about 5 percent of the chain's company-owned U.S. locations have now organized. Federal regulators have accused the company of violating labor law hundreds of times in its efforts to clamp down on union organizing. \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  has denied the accusations.\par \par \uc2 Last February, after months of stalled talks, \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  signaled a shift in its approach to the labor campaign under a newly installed chief executive, \uc2 Laxman Narasimhan\uc2 , who agreed to begin contract discussions.\par \par \uc2 But what the union viewed at the time as a notable change in strategy has failed to yield an agreement. After monthly bargaining sessions began in April, both sides said they hoped to complete a framework for a national contract by the end of the year -- a deadline they ultimate did not meet. Mr. Narasimhan left the company in August.\par \par \uc2 A framework, and subsequent contract ratifications at \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  stores across the country, would represent a notable development in labor relations in corporate America. Major companies, including \uc2 Amazon\uc2  and \uc2 Apple\uc2 , have resisted union organizing in recent years to varying degrees.\par \par \uc2 Starbucks and Workers United have reached agreements on dozens of issues related to work conditions, the union said at the end of last year. But they remain at odds on key economic provisions. In the most recent bargaining session in December, the company offered to guarantee baristas a wage increase of at least 1.5 percent a year.\par \par \uc2 ''We are optimistic that \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  will move off of their fixed position on wage and benefits improvements in this next phase of negotiations,'' Michelle Eisen, a longtime \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  barista who has helped lead the organizing, said in a statement on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 A \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  spokesman declined to comment beyond the joint statement.\par \par \uc2 In filing for union elections, \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  workers have also cited frustration over understaffing and insufficient training, problems that appeared to intensify during the pandemic. Workers United pointed to ''fair raises, benefits and staffing'' as key concerns driving the strike in December.\par \par \uc2 This week, \uc2 Brian Niccol\uc2 , who became the chain's chief executive in September, outlined strategic decisions that appear to be partly aimed at appeasing employees, most of whom have not yet unionized. He told Wall Street analysts and investors that \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  had increased work hours at 3,000 stores where he said staffing ''had gotten too thin.'' The company's moves to simplify its menu and smooth out its mobile ordering systems, he said, are partially geared toward improving work conditions.\par \par \uc2 Noam Scheiber contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Noam Scheiber contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020250201el210003v\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
