\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 Musk's 'Fork in the Road' Is One He's Used Before\b0\par\par\uc2 By Kate Conger and Ryan Mac \par \uc2 1,049 \uc2 words\par \uc2 31 January 2025\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Federal agencies have offered exits to millions of employees and tested the prowess of engineers -- just like when \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  bought \uc2 Twitter\uc2 . The similarities have been uncanny.\par \par \uc2 The email landed in employees' inboxes with the subject line: ''Fork in the Road.'' The message in the email was stark: Accept a sweeping set of workplace changes or resign.\par \par \uc2 That was the note that millions of federal employees received around 5 p.m. on Tuesday. It echoed a similar message that thousands of workers at \uc2 Twitter\uc2  got from \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  in late 2022 after he bought the company.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Musk has brought his takeover tactics from \uc2 Twitter\uc2  to the federal government, where he's become a close adviser to President Trump and is running the cost-cutting initiative called the Department of Government Efficiency. The administration is already eliminating diversity initiatives, testing engineering prowess and demanding federal employee loyalty, maneuvers Mr. Musk embraced to slash his social media company's budget and operations.\par \par \uc2 The Tuesday email, sent by the Office of Personnel Management, was the latest example of the billionaire's fingerprints, down to the subject line -- the same as his 2022 buyout offer to \uc2 Twitter\uc2  employees.\par \par \uc2 ''It's a knife-at-your-throat feeling,'' said Rumman Chowdhury, a former \uc2 Twitter\uc2  executive who left the company, which is now known as X, after Mr. Musk's takeover. ''The uncertainty is wild.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Musk and a spokeswoman for his government cost-cutting initiative did not immediately respond to requests for comment.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Musk has previously said he expects to slash $2 trillion from the federal budget. Between 5 and 10 percent of federal workers were expected to accept the exit packages offered on Tuesday, ''which could lead to around $100 billion in savings,'' Mr. Musk's political action committee, America PAC, said in a post on X.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Musk, who also leads \uc2 Tesla\uc2  and \uc2 SpaceX\uc2 , has enlisted the help of a team of loyalists to assess agencies and make cuts, the same thing he did during the \uc2 Twitter\uc2  takeover.\par \par \uc2 Steve Davis, the head of Mr. Musk's tunneling startup, \uc2 The Boring Company\uc2 , helped oversee cost-cutting at \uc2 Twitter\uc2  and now leads DOGE. Brian Bjelde, a longtime human resources executive at \uc2 SpaceX\uc2  who also helped during the \uc2 Twitter\uc2  takeover, is now an adviser to the Office of Personnel Management.\par \par \uc2 Michael Grimes, a top banker at \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  who helped lead Mr. Musk's \uc2 Twitter\uc2  acquisition, is expected to take a senior job at the Commerce Department.\par \par \uc2 One of Mr. Musk's software engineers at \uc2 Tesla\uc2 , Thomas Shedd, was named the head of ''Technology Transformation Services'' at the \uc2 General Services Administration\uc2 , which helps manage federal agencies. Mr. Shedd promptly employed a Musk tactic: asking for proof of engineers' technical chops.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Shedd asked for engineers to sign up for sessions in which they could share ''a recent individual technical win,'' according to an email sent to more than 700 employees on Tuesday night and viewed by The Times.\par \par \uc2 ''These sessions are an opportunity to understand more deeply the types of expertise we have,'' Mr. Shedd said in his email, which emphasized his ''hours on the factory floor'' at \uc2 Tesla\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Just a day after acquiring \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , Mr. Musk demanded engineers print out and share code they had written for the company's various products.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Musk also worked quickly to undo diversity initiatives at the social media company -- a move echoed by Mr. Trump's flurry of executive orders his first week in office, which eliminated diversity and inclusion efforts in the federal government.\par \par \uc2 ''These programs divided Americans by race, wasted taxpayer dollars, and resulted in shameful discrimination,'' according to emails sent to federal workers viewed by The Times.\par \par \uc2 After buying \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , Mr. Musk slammed previous management's attempts to foster diversity, sharing a video in which he mocked shirts made for the company's Black employee group. Under his leadership, the company cut funding for employee resource groups, and took down a mural celebrating the \uc2 Black Lives Matter movement\uc2  in its San Francisco office, according to two former employees.\par \par \uc2 One of Mr. Musk's go-to tactics for cutting costs at \uc2 Twitter\uc2  was reducing the company's real estate footprint. He stopped paying rent for various office spaces around the world, leading to evictions from several facilities, including in Seattle and Boulder, Colo.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 General Services Administration\uc2  is also targeting real estate for cost-cutting. Stephen Ehikian, a former tech executive and Trump appointee, told workers in an email on Tuesday viewed by The Times that two of the agency's properties would be listed for sale while three leases had been terminated. The move would save about $11 million and was a ''first step'' in slashing real estate expenditures, according to the email.\par \par \uc2 While Mr. Musk ultimately transformed \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , reducing staff by 80 percent and minimizing its real estate footprint, its business has declined. Advertisers have fled the site in droves, and at least one investor, \uc2 Fidelity\uc2 , estimates the company is now worth 72 percent less than the $44 billion he paid for it.\par \par \uc2 ''Our user growth is stagnant, revenue is unimpressive and we're barely breaking even,'' Mr. Musk wrote in an email to X employees earlier this month, which was viewed by \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  and previously reported by The Wall Street Journal. ''We need to be faster, more innovative and relentlessly focused.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Musk on Tuesday evening resurfaced one of his 2024 posts on X, appearing to reference the ''fork in the road'' email to federal employees, which demanded that they be ''reliable, loyal, trustworthy'' and ''strive for excellence.''\par \par \uc2 Some government workers are looking to former \uc2 Twitter\uc2  employees for guidance.\par \par \uc2 Several federal employees circulated online posts from Dr. Chowdhury offering advice about how to respond to a Musk takeover. Others contacted Shannon Liss-Riordan, an employment lawyer who is suing Mr. Musk on behalf of former \uc2 Twitter\uc2  workers.\par \par \uc2 ''Elon clearly thinks he learned from \uc2 Twitter\uc2  how to decimate a workplace and he's trying to roll out the same playbook with the federal government now,'' Ms. Liss-Riordan said.\par \par \uc2 Theodore Schleifer, Ana Swanson and Maureen Farrell contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Theodore Schleifer, Ana Swanson and Maureen Farrell contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020250131el1v0004a\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
