\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 After Union Gained a Foothold, \uc2 Amazon\uc2  Closes Out in Quebec\b0\par\par\uc2 By Karen Weise and Vjosa Isai \par \uc2 714 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 January 2025\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Unions had gained a foothold in one of the company's warehouses in the province, though \uc2 Amazon\uc2  would not say if there was a connection.\par \par \uc2 Amazon\uc2  on Wednesday said it was closing all of its warehouse and logistics operations in Quebec, the Canadian province where unions gained a foothold in one of its facilities, and would lay off 1,700 employees.\par \par \uc2 The closures represent a U-turn from \uc2 Amazon\uc2 's recent investments in the province. The company opened three delivery stations in 2021, and one last year. It also had a small fulfillment center in Quebec and two warehouses that sorted packages.\par \par \uc2 All told, the investments totaled about 2 million square feet of operations, according to an estimate by \uc2 Marc Wulfraat\uc2 , a warehousing industry consultant based in Montreal who has long researched \uc2 Amazon\uc2 's logistics network.\par \par \uc2 Amazon\uc2  said it is closing the seven facilities to ''provide the same great service and even more savings to our customers over the long run,'' according to a statement from Barbara Agrait, a company spokeswoman.\par \par \uc2 Amazon\uc2  will still serve customers in Quebec by returning to its operational model from before 2020, when facilities in neighboring provinces prepared the packages that were then carried by third-party delivery companies into Quebec.\par \par \uc2 Amazon\uc2 's first union in Canada comprised about 230 warehouse workers in Laval, north of Montreal, after they unionized in May. But the company challenged the unionization effort before a provincial labor tribunal. It argued that the union certification should be revoked because the workers signed union cards to signal their support, instead of voting by secret ballot. The tribunal ruled against \uc2 Amazon\uc2  in October, just before the peak holiday shopping season.\par \par \uc2 Amazon\uc2  said litigation over the matter was continuing.\par \par \uc2 With the Quebec closures, ''they made it very clear we do not want this spreading,'' Mr. Wulfraat said, referring to the union effort. The company has more than 46,000 corporate and operations employees in Canada.\par \par \uc2 Fran\u231\'00\'E7ois-Philippe Champagne\uc2 , the federal innovation minister, said in a post on X that he had conveyed his disappointment to the head of \uc2 Amazon\uc2  in Canada.\par \par \uc2 ''This is not the way business is done in Canada,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 The Conf\u233\'00\'E9d\u233\'00\'E9ration des Syndicats Nationaux, a union representing the workers, said it was informed of the closures through an email from one of \uc2 Amazon\uc2 's lawyers early this morning. Caroline Senneville, the confederation's president, said in a statement that the company had been stifling their union drive since it began three years ago, through actions that included what she called ''disguised dismissals.''\par \par \uc2 ''It's a slap in the face for all workers in Quebec,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 Amazon\uc2  denied claims from the union that the dismissals were improper.\par \par \uc2 The Montreal metropolitan area has roughly 4.5 million residents, making it larger than the greater Seattle region. Pulling operations out of a major population center is contrary to what \uc2 Amazon\uc2  has touted in recent years as a central driver of success within its operations: putting more products closer to customers, to enable faster delivery. That, \uc2 Amazon\uc2  has repeatedly said, drives down delivery costs, and causes customers to order more frequently.\par \par \uc2 Amazon\uc2  has not abandoned direct operations from a large population center in North America in years, though more than a dozen years ago it routinely played hardball with states that tried to collect taxes for online sales.\par \par \uc2 Walmart\uc2  and other retailers in the past have had difficulty establishing a logistics foothold in Quebec, where roughly two out of every five workers are unionized. That's the highest rate among Canadian provinces, according to government data, and about four times as high as in the United States.\par \par \uc2 Fran\u231\'00\'E7ois Legault\uc2 , the premier of Quebec, said \uc2 Amazon\uc2 's move was ''a private decision by a private company.''\par \par \uc2 ''I can understand that it must be tough for the 1,700 families involved,'' Mr. Legault told reporters at a news conference on Wednesday, focusing most of his remarks on the need for Quebecers to mobilize and buy local products in response to President Trump's tariff threat.\par \par \uc2 Jean Boulet, the province's labor minister, said workers affected by the warehouse shutdowns would receive assistance from the government to find new jobs.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020250124el1o00036\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
