\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 Meta\uc2  to Cut 5% of Workers In a New Round of Layoffs\b0\par\par\uc2 By Kate Conger, Mike Isaac and Sheera Frenkel \par \uc2 783 \uc2 words\par \uc2 15 January 2025\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 5\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The layoffs come as \uc2 Mark Zuckerberg\uc2  pushes to remake his company for the Trump era and prepares to co-host a gala for the president-elect's inauguration.\par \par \uc2 Meta\uc2  plans to lay off up to 5 percent of its employees based on performance ratings, according to an internal memo to workers on Tuesday viewed by \uc2 The New York Times\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ''I've decided to raise the bar on performance management and move out low-performers faster,'' \uc2 Mark Zuckerberg\uc2 , chief executive of \uc2 Meta\uc2 , said in the memo. ''We typically manage out people who aren't meeting expectations over the course of a year, but now we're going to do more extensive performance-based cuts during this cycle.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Zuckerberg said in the memo that workers whose roles were cut would be replaced by new hires in 2025.\par \par \uc2 The layoffs came days after \uc2 Meta\uc2  announced sweeping changes to its content moderation policies. The company, which owns \uc2 Facebook\uc2 , \uc2 Instagram\uc2 , WhatsApp and Threads, said it would no longer police certain types of hate speech, including allowing users of its apps to suggest that L.G.B.T.Q. identities are rooted in mental illness.\par \par \uc2 Meta\uc2  also said it would stop fact-checking posts and promote political news in its Newsfeed, reversing several of its content moderation rules in preparation for the incoming Trump administration. President-elect Donald J. Trump has criticized \uc2 Meta\uc2  and other tech companies for what he describes as censorship of conservative viewpoints.\par \par \uc2 A spokesman for \uc2 Meta\uc2  declined to comment on the layoffs. \uc2 Bloomberg\uc2  earlier reported on the cuts.\par \par \uc2 Last week, Mr. Zuckerberg said the company was terminating its diversity, equity and inclusion programs, effective immediately. In an interview with the podcaster Joe Rogan on Friday, Mr. Zuckerberg also said that ''masculine energy, I think, is good.''\par \par \uc2 ''It's like, you want feminine energy, you want masculine energy,'' Mr. Zuckerberg said. ''I think that that's all good. But I do think the corporate culture sort of had swung toward being this somewhat more neutered thing.''\par \par \uc2 In \uc2 Meta\uc2 's internal company message boards, employees asked whether the cuts would target specific groups, such as the L.G.B.T.Q. community or people of color.\par \par \uc2 ''Given what we heard Mark say about D.E.I. last week, do we think these cuts are coming for people who don't have the masculine energy he is looking for?'' one employee asked.\par \par \uc2 In a separate memo to managers viewed by The Times, \uc2 Meta\uc2  said that the cuts were to ensure the company had the ''strongest talent'' working at the company, and that it would give \uc2 Meta\uc2  the ability to hire new workers. Managers were also told that those laid off would receive ''generous'' severance packages.\par \par \uc2 The changes coincide with Mr. Zuckerberg's broader push to remake his company for the Trump era and protect \uc2 Meta\uc2  from the threat of regulation by forging closer bonds with the incoming administration.\par \par \uc2 Last year, Mr. Zuckerberg made his first visit to Mar-a-Lago, Mr. Trump's private club in Palm Beach, Fla. Mr. Zuckerberg donated $1 million to Mr. Trump's inaugural fund, and he has embraced working with the administration on fighting against what he describes as unduly harsh regulation in the \uc2 European Union\uc2  and other overseas markets.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Zuckerberg also plans to attend Mr. Trump's inauguration ceremony on Jan. 20, according to two people familiar with his plans who spoke on the condition of anonymity. And he will co-host a black-tie reception for the incoming president that evening, along with the Republican megadonors Miriam Adelson and Tilman Fertitta, according to a copy of the invitation shared with The Times.\par \par \uc2 A spokesman for \uc2 Meta\uc2  declined to comment on Mr. Zuckerberg's political activities.\par \par \uc2 Meta\uc2  has more than 72,000 employees, according to its latest earnings report, and the cuts Mr. Zuckerberg announced on Tuesday would eliminate about 3,600 people.\par \par \uc2 The cuts also represent \uc2 Meta\uc2 's first major culling since 2023, when Mr. Zuckerberg embarked upon what he called the ''year of efficiency,'' a move to thin his work force after years of what he said was ''over hiring'' during the pandemic. Mr. Zuckerberg pressured managers across departments to set higher performance goals to weed out low performers, resulting in \uc2 Meta\uc2 's cutting roughly one-third of its overall work force in 2023.\par \par \uc2 Still, \uc2 Meta\uc2 's ranks have grown. Mr. Zuckerberg has aimed to replace many of those employees with new hires focused on artificial intelligence, as \uc2 Meta\uc2  and other big tech companies focus on developing chatbots and other A.I.-powered services.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020250115el1f0004i\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
