\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 Labor; Mastercard Opts to Settle Pay Discrimination Suit\b0\par\par\uc2 By Danielle Kaye \par \uc2 413 \uc2 words\par \uc2 15 January 2025\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The complaint accuses the company of underpaying female, Black and Hispanic employees compared with their male and white counterparts.\par \par \uc2 Mastercard\uc2  is set to pay $26 million to settle accusations that the company has for years underpaid thousands of female, Black and Hispanic employees, according to an agreement filed in court on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 The settlement, if approved by the U.S. District Court for the Southern District of New York, will resolve claims that \uc2 Mastercard\uc2  underpaid roughly 7,500 employees across the country starting in 2016. The company, which did not admit to any wrongdoing, also agreed to conduct annual pay equity audits for three years and hire a psychologist to assess bias in its workplace.\par \par \uc2 Female, Black and Hispanic employees were paid less than their male and white counterparts for doing the same or similar work, according to the proposed class action complaint, which was also filed on Tuesday. \uc2 Mastercard\uc2 , the world's second-largest payment processing company, on several occasions improperly assigned women and people of color to job levels below their experience, contributing to pay gaps, the complaint says.\par \par \uc2 ''We think it's a good resolution for the clients, and we think it provides a framework for other companies in the industry to improve their practices with respect to pay equity,'' said Cara Greene, a partner at the law firm Outten & Golden who is representing the plaintiffs in the case.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Greene called the settlement a ''fair compromise'' that reflected the claims from the thousands of women and people of color who had worked at \uc2 Mastercard\uc2 . She declined to share specific pay discrepancy figures.\par \par \uc2 A spokesman for \uc2 Mastercard\uc2 , \uc2 Seth Eisen\uc2 , said that the company ''strongly'' denied the allegations in the lawsuit, and that in settling the case, \uc2 Mastercard\uc2  was ''committing to continue to support and deliver for our employees.''\par \par \uc2 He added, ''It is in the best interest of all involved to bring this matter to a close.''\par \par \uc2 Other major companies, including those in the financial services industry, have settled pay discrimination claims in recent years, in some cases paying significantly larger sums. In 2023, \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  agreed to pay $215 million to settle a gender bias lawsuit that accused the bank of hindering women's career advancement and paying them less than their male colleagues. In 2022, \uc2 Google\uc2  agreed to pay $118 million to settle a class-action lawsuit that accused it of systematically underpaying women.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020250115el1f0004h\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
