\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Say Anything: Remaking Meta For Trump Era\b0\par\par\uc2 By Mike Isaac, Sheera Frenkel and Kate Conger \par \uc2 1,770 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 January 2025\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 After visiting President-elect \uc2 Donald J. Trump\uc2  in November, Mr. Zuckerberg decided to relax \uc2 Meta\uc2 's speech policies. He asked a small team to carry out his goals within weeks. The repercussions are just beginning.\par \par \uc2 Mark Zuckerberg\uc2  kept the circle of people who knew his thinking small.\par \par \uc2 Last month, Mr. Zuckerberg, the chief executive of \uc2 Meta\uc2 , tapped a handful of top policy and communications executives and others to discuss the company's approach to online speech. He had decided to make sweeping changes after visiting President-elect \uc2 Donald J. Trump\uc2  at Mar-a-Lago over Thanksgiving. Now he needed his employees to turn those changes into policy.\par \par \uc2 Over the next few weeks, Mr. Zuckerberg and his handpicked team discussed how to do that in \uc2 Zoom\uc2  meetings, conference calls and late-night group chats. Some subordinates stole away from family dinners and holiday gatherings to work, while Mr. Zuckerberg weighed in between trips to his homes in the San Francisco Bay Area and the island of Kauai.\par \par \uc2 By New Year's Day, Mr. Zuckerberg was ready to go public with the changes, according to four current and former \uc2 Meta\uc2  employees and advisers with knowledge of the events, who were not authorized to speak publicly about the confidential discussions.\par \par \uc2 The entire process was highly unusual. \uc2 Meta\uc2  typically alters policies that govern its apps -- which include \uc2 Facebook\uc2 , \uc2 Instagram\uc2 , WhatsApp and Threads -- by inviting employees, civic leaders and others to weigh in. Any shifts generally take months. But Mr. Zuckerberg turned this latest effort into a closely held six-week sprint, blindsiding even employees on his policy and integrity teams.\par \par \uc2 On Tuesday, most of \uc2 Meta\uc2 's 72,000 employees learned of Mr. Zuckerberg's plans along with the rest of the world. The Silicon Valley giant said it was overhauling speech on its apps by loosening restrictions on how people can talk about contentious social issues such as immigration, gender and sexuality. It killed its fact-checking program that had been aimed at curbing misinformation and said it would instead rely on users to police falsehoods. And it said it would insert more political content into people's feeds after previously de-emphasizing that very material.\par \par \uc2 In the days since, the moves -- which have sweeping implications for what people will see online -- have drawn applause from Mr. Trump and conservatives, criticism from President Biden, derision from fact-checking groups and misinformation researchers, and concerns from L.G.B.T.Q. advocacy groups that fear the changes will lead to more people getting harassed online and offline.\par \par \uc2 Inside Meta, the reaction has been sharply divided. Some employees have celebrated the moves, while others were shocked and have openly castigated the changes on the company's internal message boards. Several employees wrote that they were ashamed to work for \uc2 Meta\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 On Friday, \uc2 Meta\uc2 's makeover continued when the company told employees that it would end its work on diversity, equity and inclusion. It eliminated its chief diversity officer role, ended its diversity hiring goals that called for the employment of a certain number of women and minorities, and said it would no longer prioritize minority-owned businesses when hiring vendors.\par \par \uc2 Meta\uc2  planned to ''focus on how to apply fair and consistent practices that mitigate bias for all, no matter your background,'' Janelle Gale, vice president of human resources, said in an internal post that was relayed to \uc2 The New York Times\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 At the White House on Friday, President Biden told reporters that Mr. Zuckerberg's decision to abandon fact-checking on \uc2 Facebook\uc2  and \uc2 Instagram\uc2  was ''shameful.''\par \par \uc2 In interviews, more than a dozen current and former \uc2 Meta\uc2  employees, executives and advisers to Mr. Zuckerberg described his shift as serving a dual purpose. It positions \uc2 Meta\uc2  for the political landscape of the moment, with conservative power ascendant in Washington as Mr. Trump takes office on Jan. 20. More than that, the changes reflect Mr. Zuckerberg's personal views of how his $1.5 trillion company should be run -- and he no longer wants to keep those views quiet.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Zuckerberg, 40, has regularly spoken to friends and colleagues, including \uc2 Marc Andreessen\uc2 , the venture capitalist and \uc2 Meta\uc2  board member, about concerns that progressives are policing speech, the people said. He has also felt railroaded by what he views as the Biden administration's anti-tech posturing, and stung by what he sees as progressives in the media and in Silicon Valley -- including in \uc2 Meta\uc2 's work force -- pushing him to take a heavy hand in policing discourse, they said.\par \par \uc2 Meta\uc2  declined to comment.\par \par \uc2 In an interview with the podcaster Joe Rogan on Friday, Mr. Zuckerberg said it was time to go ''back to our original mission'' by giving people ''the power to share.'' He said he had felt pressured by the Biden administration and the media to ''censor'' certain content, adding, ''I have a much greater command now of what I think the policy should be, and this is how it's going to be going forward.''\par \par \uc2 The latest changes were catalyzed by Mr. Trump's victory in November. That month, Mr. Zuckerberg flew to Florida to meet with Mr. Trump at Mar-a-Lago. \uc2 Meta\uc2  later donated $1 million to the president-elect's inaugural fund.\par \par \uc2 At \uc2 Meta\uc2 , Mr. Zuckerberg began preparing to change speech policies. Knowing that any moves would be contentious, he assembled a team of no more than a dozen close advisers and lieutenants, including \uc2 Joel Kaplan\uc2 , a longtime policy executive with strong ties to the Republican Party; Kevin Martin, the head of U.S. policy; and \uc2 David Ginsberg\uc2 , the head of communications. Mr. Zuckerberg insisted on no leaks, the people with knowledge of the effort said.\par \par \uc2 The group worked on revising \uc2 Meta\uc2 's ''Hate Speech'' policy, with Mr. Zuckerberg leading the charge, they said. They changed the name of the policy, which lays out what to do with slurs, threats against protected groups and other harmful content on its apps, to ''Hateful Conduct.''\par \par \uc2 That effectively shifted the emphasis of the rules away from speech, minimizing \uc2 Meta\uc2 's role in policing online conversation. Mr. Kaplan and Mr. Martin were cheerleaders of the changes, these people said.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Zuckerberg decided to promote Mr. Kaplan to \uc2 Meta\uc2 's head of global public policy to carry out the changes and deepen \uc2 Meta\uc2 's ties to the incoming Trump administration, replacing \uc2 Nick Clegg\uc2 , a former deputy prime minister of Britain who had handled policy and regulatory issues globally for \uc2 Meta\uc2  since 2018. The night before \uc2 Meta\uc2 's announcement, Mr. Kaplan held individual calls with top conservative social media influencers, two people said.\par \par \uc2 On Tuesday, Mr. Zuckerberg made the new speech policies public in his \uc2 Instagram\uc2  video. Mr. Kaplan appeared on ''Fox & Friends,'' a mainstay of Mr. Trump's media diet, saying \uc2 Meta\uc2 's fact-checking partners ''had too much political bias.''\par \par \uc2 (Fact-checking groups that worked with \uc2 Meta\uc2  have said they had no role in deciding what the company did with the content that was fact-checked.)\par \par \uc2 Among its changes, \uc2 Meta\uc2  loosened rules so people could post statements saying they hated people of certain races, religions or sexual orientations, including permitting ''allegations of mental illness or abnormality when based on gender or sexual orientation.'' The company cited political discourse about transgender rights for the change. It also removed a rule that forbade users to say people of certain races were responsible for spreading the coronavirus.\par \par \uc2 Some training materials that \uc2 Meta\uc2  created for the new policies were confusing and contradictory, two employees who reviewed the documents said. Some of the text said saying that ''white people have mental illness'' would be prohibited on \uc2 Facebook\uc2 , but saying that ''gay people have mental illness'' was allowed, they said.\par \par \uc2 Meta\uc2  locked access to the policies and training materials internally late on Thursday, they said, hours after The Intercept published excerpts.\par \par \uc2 The company also removed the transgender and nonbinary ''themes'' on its Messenger chat app, which allows users to customize the app's colors and wallpaper, two employees said. The change was reported earlier by 404 Media.\par \par \uc2 That same day at \uc2 Meta\uc2 's offices in Silicon Valley, Texas and New York, facilities managers were instructed to remove tampons from men's bathrooms, which the company had provided for nonbinary and transgender employees who use the men's room and who may have required sanitary pads, two employees said.\par \par \uc2 Some employees were livid at what they saw as efforts by executives to hide changes to the ''Hateful Conduct'' policy before it was announced, two people said. While people across the policy division typically view and comment on significant revisions, most did not have the opportunity this time.\par \par \uc2 On Workplace, \uc2 Meta\uc2 's Slack-like internal communications software, employees began arguing over the changes. In the @Pride employee resource group, where workers who support L.G.B.T.Q. issues convene, at least one person announced their resignation as others privately relayed to one another that they planned to look for jobs elsewhere, two people said.\par \par \uc2 In a post this week to the @Pride group, \uc2 Alex Schultz\uc2 , \uc2 Meta\uc2 's chief marketing officer, defended Mr. Zuckerberg and said topics like transgender issues had become politicized. He said \uc2 Meta\uc2 's policies should not get in the way of allowing societal debate and pointed to Roe v. Wade, the landmark abortion case, as an example of ''courts getting ahead of society'' in the 1970s. Mr. Schultz said the courts had ''politicized'' the issue instead of allowing it to be debated civically.\par \par \uc2 ''You find topics become politicized and stay in the political conversation for far longer than they would've if society just debated them out,'' Mr. Schultz wrote. He said looser restrictions on speech in \uc2 Meta\uc2 's apps would allow for this kind of debate.\par \par \uc2 On Friday, Roy Austin, \uc2 Meta\uc2 's vice president of civil rights, announced he was leaving the company. He did not give a reason.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Zuckerberg traveled to Palm Beach, Fla., this week, four people with knowledge of his activities said, and on Friday was said to have been at Mar-a-Lago.\par \par \uc2 In his interview with Mr. Rogan, Mr. Zuckerberg denied making sweeping changes to appease the incoming Trump administration, but said the election did influence his thinking.\par \par \uc2 ''The good thing about doing it after the election is you get to take this cultural pulse,'' he said. ''We got to this point where there were these things that you couldn't say that were just mainstream discourse.''\par \par \uc2 Theodore Schleifer, Maggie Haberman and Jonathan Swan contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Theodore Schleifer, Maggie Haberman and Jonathan Swan contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020250112el1c0004l\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
