\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 Baristas at More Than 300 Starbucks Stores Walk Out, Union Says\b0\par\par\uc2 By Noam Scheiber \par \uc2 639 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 December 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The strike began Friday at stores in the Los Angeles, Seattle and Chicago areas and will end on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 A union representing more than 10,000 \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  employees said a strike that began Friday had expanded to include more than 300 stores on Tuesday, the planned final day of a five-day walkout.\par \par \uc2 Workers in about 10 to 15 stores across the Los Angeles, Seattle and Chicago areas kicked off the strike, which spread to a similar number of stores in three additional metropolitan areas each day. By Monday, baristas in more than 50 stores in cities like Boston, Dallas, Denver, New York and Philadelphia did not report for work.\par \par \uc2 The strike spread to additional cities on Tuesday, union representatives said, as well as to additional stores in cities where some baristas were already on strike, potentially dampening business at one of the company's busiest times of the year. Closed stores are expected to reopen on Wednesday, when workers have said they would return to work.\par \par \uc2 A typical company-owned \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  store in the United States brings in sales of about $4,000 to $8,000 during a normal business day, according to a former company official and a review of company financial filings. The figures tend to be higher in December.\par \par \uc2 A \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  spokesman said on Monday he could not confirm the sales figure.\par \par \uc2 The union, Workers United, represents \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  employees at more than 500 company-owned stores across the country, about 5 percent of the U.S. total. It said it called the strike because \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  had yet to resolve more than 150 unfair-labor-practice charges on issues like retaliatory firings and cuts to hours, and because \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  had not offered a substantial wage increase during contract negotiations.\par \par \uc2 In the most recent bargaining session, which took place last week, the company offered to guarantee baristas a wage increase of at least 1.5 percent a year. If the company raised wages for all retail employees more than that -- as it did with a recently announced 2 percent increase for the least experienced workers -- unionized baristas would get the higher amount.\par \par \uc2 ''After all \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  has said about how they value partners throughout the system, we refuse to accept zero immediate investment in baristas' wages,'' \uc2 Lynne Fox\uc2 , the president of Workers United, said in a statement. ''Union baristas know their value, and they're not going to accept a proposal that doesn't treat them as true partners.''\par \par \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  said in a statement on Monday that ''the overwhelming majority of \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  stores across the country have opened as planned'' during the past few days, adding that the company is ''ready to continue negotiations when the union comes back to the bargaining table.''\par \par \uc2 It is unclear how many of the striking stores have been shut down. The company said Tuesday morning that about 170 stores ''did not open as planned,'' while the union said at least 290 locations had closed by noon.\par \par \uc2 The union campaign at \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  began in the Buffalo area in 2021 and spread across the country the next year. \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  had largely resisted union organizing by its baristas until February of this year, when it negotiated a peace deal of sorts with the union.\par \par \uc2 The two sides agreed to a process that would allow baristas to unionize without opposition from the company, and to monthly bargaining sessions to negotiate a national contract framework. The sessions began in April, and both sides said they hoped to complete the framework by the end of the year.\par \par \uc2 The two sides also agreed to work to resolve lawsuits and formal accusations of labor law violations that had accumulated during the union campaign. They have resolved hundreds of such cases so far.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020241225ekcp0003t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
