\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 Staten Island \uc2 Amazon\uc2  Workers Join Labor Action\b0\par\par\uc2 By Santul Nerkar and Noam Scheiber \par \uc2 845 \uc2 words\par \uc2 23 December 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The workers' union hopes that adding employees at the Staten Island warehouse to a protest started by delivery drivers will increase pressure on \uc2 Amazon\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Signaling an escalation in a labor campaign that began at seven \uc2 Amazon\uc2  delivery hubs on Thursday, workers at the company's largest Staten Island warehouse began a protest there at midnight on Saturday.\par \par \uc2 By late morning, a group of around 100 people -- a small percentage of the more than 5,000 workers at that warehouse -- had gathered outside. Union organizers had set up tents, food stations and a heater next to a bus station across the street from the warehouse. Many of the workers said they had been scheduled to work that day and did not clock in, while others said they had not been scheduled to work.\par \par \uc2 They were joined by New York's attorney general, Letitia James, who spoke briefly and told the workers that ''the law is on your side.''\par \par \uc2 ''I want all of you to know that \uc2 Amazon\uc2  can't just share the benefits of your hard work at the top,'' Ms. James said. ''They need to make sure that you are being paid.''\par \par \uc2 The protest during the holiday rush raises questions about how much of an impact it will have on \uc2 Amazon\uc2 's operations. Similar questions have accompanied walkouts led by the \uc2 International Brotherhood of Teamsters\uc2  at delivery hubs in New York, Georgia, Illinois and California, which began Thursday.\par \par \uc2 Kelly Nantel, a spokeswoman for \uc2 Amazon\uc2 , said the protest had not affected operations at the Staten Island warehouse. On Saturday, \uc2 Amazon\uc2  delivery trucks continued to roll in and out of the Staten Island warehouse with ease, although some did honk in support of the workers.\par \par \uc2 The Staten Island warehouse, known as JFK8, employs by far the largest group at \uc2 Amazon\uc2  who have sought to be represented by the \uc2 Teamsters\uc2 . That makes it the union's greatest potential source of leverage as the \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  tries to pressure \uc2 Amazon\uc2  to bargain with drivers and other workers who have organized.\par \par \uc2 The workers who joined the labor action said they wanted \uc2 Amazon\uc2  to provide better pay, sick leave and working conditions.\par \par \uc2 ''\uc2 Amazon\uc2  is jeopardizing the holidays for consumers so they can try to make an extra buck,'' Connor Spence, president of the local chapter representing workers at JFK8, said in a statement. ''\uc2 Amazon\uc2  workers are standing up to demand this corporation finally treat them with respect.''\par \par \uc2 Unlike the drivers the \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  have tried to unionize at delivery hubs, the Staten Island workers are employed by \uc2 Amazon\uc2  directly rather than through contractors. That gives them a somewhat stronger legal foothold for challenging the company.\par \par \uc2 Anthony Grant, who has worked at JFK8 for four years, said he did not show up for his typical shift on Saturday, which starts at 6:15 a.m.\par \par \uc2 ''\uc2 Amazon\uc2  is not a bad place to be, but they fall short on a lot of things,'' said Mr. Grant, who earns roughly $24 an hour for processing damaged goods. He said he was striking for better pay and workplace conditions.\par \par \uc2 Thomas Clowery, who processes, stamps and stows items at JFK8, has worked at the warehouse since it opened in 2018 and said he was striking for better pay and benefits.\par \par \uc2 ''They're way underpaying us,'' said Mr. Clowery, who had been scheduled to work an additional holiday shift on Saturday that was mandatory. He said that the $24 hourly wage he earned wasn't enough to live comfortably in Staten Island.\par \par \uc2 Seth Siegel, 65, who also stows items at the warehouse, said the improvements he was seeking were ''comprehensive,'' including more break time, vacation days and pay.\par \par \uc2 ''\uc2 Amazon\uc2  won't sit down and negotiate with us,'' said Mr. Siegel, who was not scheduled to work on Saturday. ''Right now, it's just take it or leave it, and some of it we feel should be negotiated.''\par \par \uc2 The JFK8 workers joined a union called the Amazon Labor Union when they voted to unionize in 2022. The A.L.U. was initially independent, and it struggled to sustain the support of workers in the warehouse and to secure gains from \uc2 Amazon\uc2 , which challenged the election outcome and has refused to recognize or bargain with the union. (The \uc2 National Labor Relations Board\uc2  certified the election, but a federal court paused further action on the case while the company challenged the constitutionality of the board.)\par \par \uc2 In June, the A.L.U. affiliated with the \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  under an agreement that gave the new A.L.U. local the exclusive right within the \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  to organize \uc2 Amazon\uc2  warehouses in New York City. The agreement also promised that the international union would help the local with organizing, research, communications and legal matters.\par \par \uc2 At the time, A.L.U. leaders said that the \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  told them that the international union had allocated $8 million for organizing efforts at \uc2 Amazon\uc2 , and that the \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  were also prepared to tap a strike fund of more than $300 million to support the effort.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020241223ekcn00039\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
