\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  Workers Walk Out in 3 Cities After a Failure to Agree on Raises\b0\par\par\uc2 By Noam Scheiber \par \uc2 937 \uc2 words\par \uc2 21 December 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The walkouts in Los Angeles, Chicago and Seattle come after talks between the company and the workers' union failed to produce an agreement on raises.\par \par \uc2 A union representing \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  workers said that baristas in Los Angeles, Chicago and Seattle walked off the job Friday morning and that the strikes would spread to hundreds of stores by Tuesday, Christmas Eve, unless the company improved its wage offer in contract negotiations.\par \par \uc2 The union, which represents baristas at more than 500 company-owned stores in the United States -- about 5 percent of the U.S. total -- said it called the strike after a bargaining session with the company this week failed to produce better wage gains.\par \par \uc2 The strike appeared to affect about 10 to 15 stores across the three metropolitan areas, according to union and company officials. .\par \par \uc2 ''\uc2 Starbucks\uc2  proposed an economic package with no new wage increases for union baristas now and a guarantee of only 1.5 percent in future years,'' the union, Workers United, said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 The guarantee would entitle unionized \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  workers to receive a wage increase of 1.5 percent even if the company raises wages nationwide by less than that amount in future years. If the company raised wages by more -- as it did this year, with a recently announced increase of 2 percent -- unionized workers would get the higher amount.\par \par \uc2 Andrew Trull, a \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  spokesman, said union delegates ''prematurely ended'' this week's negotiations. ''It is disappointing they didn't return to the table given the progress we've made to date,'' he added.\par \par \uc2 The union's website says it is seeking a base wage of at least $20 an hour for all baristas. Mr. Trull said the company's current wage floor in the United States is $15.25, and that the average hourly wage in stores is more than $18.\par \par \uc2 The two sides have been bargaining a national contract framework during monthly sessions since April. They have reached more than two dozen tentative agreements on a variety of issues, including health and safety, attendance policies and ensuring that workers can be fired only for just cause.\par \par \uc2 The union workers will also receive an expansion of paid parental leave that \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  recently announced for all workers; union members say the announcement appeared to be a response to a demand the union made during bargaining.\par \par \uc2 While \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  workers have waged one- and two-day strikes since they began to unionize in the fall of 2021, the latest action could see workers at dozens or even hundreds of stores walk off the job for longer periods. The union said this strike would end within five days.\par \par \uc2 About 10 workers gathered at a closed \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  Friday morning in the Edgewater neighborhood on the North Side of Chicago. They held signs that said, ''Please, Mr. Scrooge, give us a living wage,'' and ''Jingle bells, coffee sells, so why are we all broke?'' The picket grew to more than two dozen by midday, though some of the picketers appeared to be supporters rather than workers.\par \par \uc2 A second store on the North Side was also closed, as was a store in Evanston, a suburb just north of the city.\par \par \uc2 Connor Brennan, the Evanston store's delegate to the contract negotiation, said that ''the deal the company was offering us just wasn't close to where it needs to be'' when it came to wages and certain benefits, and that the decision to strike was ''pretty much unanimous.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Brennan, who arrived at the store at 5:30 a.m., said that sustaining a picket for all five days would be a challenge for his store, which he said had only nine union employees at the moment, but that the workers were up for it. ''It's small, but people are willing to stay out a long time,'' Mr. Brennan added.\par \par \uc2 The company had largely resisted the union's organizing efforts for more than two years until February, when it sought a peace deal of sorts that would establish a process for workers to unionize without opposition from the company, and another process for bargaining contracts at hundreds of stores. The two sides also agreed to try to resolve litigation that had been filed during the fight.\par \par \uc2 The decision to engage with the union was made by the company's former chief executive, \uc2 Laxman Narasimhan\uc2 , who faced pressure from a union-backed campaign to elect three new candidates to the company's board, as well as from boycotts and protests tied to the war between Israel and the Palestinians.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Narasimhan said on an earnings call in January that the protests were having a ''negative impact'' on the company financially despite being ''driven by misperceptions'' that the company was hostile to Palestinians and their supporters.\par \par \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  ousted Mr. Narasimhan in August and replaced him with \uc2 Brian Niccol\uc2 , the former chief executive of Chipotle. Mr. Niccol wrote a letter to the union in September saying he was ''committed to making sure we engage constructively and in good faith.'' But union members involved in the bargaining say the momentum slowed somewhat during the fall.\par \par \uc2 ''Our C.E.O. Brian doesn't know what it's like to preside over the company while it's fighting with us,'' said a \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  barista and bargaining delegate, Silvia Baldwin, on a call open to supporters and the media on Thursday night.\par \par \uc2 ''It was pretty clear very quickly that we were going to have to remind him or teach him what that looks like.''\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020241221ekcl0000e\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
