\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 Volkswagen\uc2  Reaches Labor Deal, Avoiding Plant Closures\b0\par\par\uc2 By Melissa Eddy \par \uc2 860 \uc2 words\par \uc2 21 December 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 5\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The automaker agreed to keep all 10 of its factories in Germany open and to guarantee workers' jobs until the end of 2030.\par \par \uc2 Volkswagen\uc2 , Germany's largest automaker, reached an agreement with its union on Friday, ending a three-month standoff that had rattled the country and brought to light the extent of German industrial decline.\par \par \uc2 Under the deal, hammered out during more than 70 hours of talks at a hotel in the western city of Hanover, the company agreed to keep all 10 of its factories in Germany open and to guarantee workers' jobs until the end of 2030. But during that time, the company will carry out a restructuring plan that will include the loss of more than 35,000 jobs through retirement and attrition.\par \par \uc2 In exchange, the union, \uc2 IG Metall\uc2 , which represents most of \uc2 Volkswagen\uc2 's workers, withdrew its demand for a wage increase until 2031 and agreed to cuts in bonuses and reductions in production of hundreds of thousands of cars across several plants.\par \par \uc2 ''No site will be closed, no one will be made redundant, and our company wage agreement will be secured for the long term,'' \uc2 Daniela Cavallo\uc2 , the head of the works council at \uc2 Volkswagen\uc2 , said in a statement announcing the agreement.\par \par \uc2 At stake was not just wages but \uc2 Volkswagen\uc2 's plans to solve the problem of too many workers and too few orders, amid slumping demand in Europe and abroad and increasing competition from Chinese automakers.\par \par \uc2 Volkswagen\uc2 's woes have mirrored Germany's economic malaise. Officials in the country have forecast that the economy will contract in 2024, for the second consecutive year. Economists do not expect to see a significant return to growth in 2025.\par \par \uc2 The extended wage dispute had strained the 87-year-old automaker's strong ties with its roughly 120,000 workers in Germany, who said management had betrayed the social pact of the country's traditional collective bargaining system, where the welfare of the company and its employees are given equal weight.\par \par \uc2 Some 100,000 \uc2 IG Metall\uc2  members staged brief walkouts in December. The union had threatened to start longer strikes starting in January if an agreement had not been reached.\par \par \uc2 That pressure, combined with \uc2 Volkswagen\uc2 's plunging share price, which has fallen by more than a third over the past 12 months, was enough to force the company to back down from its threats to close as many as three plants. \uc2 Volkswagen\uc2  had also initially sought a 10 percent wage cut.\par \par \uc2 ''With the package of measures achieved, the company has set a decisive course for its future in terms of costs, capacities and structures,'' \uc2 Oliver Blume\uc2 , the chief executive of \uc2 Volkswagen\uc2 , said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 For its part, the union retreated from its demand of a 7 percent wage increase and offered a compromise instead: the creation of a communal fund equivalent to a 5 percent wage increase for each worker over the next five years. The fund can be drawn from to compensate those employees whose hours are reduced or who are forced into early retirement through the restructuring.\par \par \uc2 Volkswagen\uc2  said the deal would save more than 4 billion euros, or roughly $4.2 billion, annually in the coming years. It would also reduce its production capacity by 734,000 cars over the coming five years, allowing the company to invest in new products that it hopes will restore its ability to compete.\par \par \uc2 ''I think it sends a good signal to the work force and creates confidence that the employees are valued,'' said Beatrix C. Keim, director of the Center for Automotive Research in Duisburg, adding that it was good for Germany's auto industry because many suppliers and their employees depend on \uc2 Volkswagen\uc2 's success.\par \par \uc2 The agreement was a sorely needed positive end to a difficult year, coming days after Chancellor \uc2 Olaf Scholz\uc2  of Germany lost a vote of confidence in Parliament, setting off new elections in February that are shaping up to focus largely on how to jump-start the country's stagnant economy.\par \par \uc2 High energy prices, a complex bureaucracy, aging public infrastructure and geopolitical developments have hurt exports, while political paralysis under Mr. Scholz's government exacerbated the situation.\par \par \uc2 The threat of further protectionism from the administration of President-elect Donald J. Trump in the United States has economists worried that the coming year could be more challenging, even if a new \uc2 German government\uc2  is able to quickly enact some badly needed reforms.\par \par \uc2 Volkswagen\uc2  is struggling against falling demand in Europe and China, the world's largest car market, and is burdened by high costs for labor and electricity, especially in Germany. In October, the company reported that profit fell to \u8364\'20\'AC2.86 billion, or $3.1 billion, for the months from August to September, its lowest level in three years.\par \par \uc2 On Friday, \uc2 Volkswagen\uc2 's top shareholder, \uc2 Porsche\uc2 , said it might have to write down the value of its 31.9 percent stake in the company by as much as \u8364\'20\'AC20 billion, or $21 billion, creating extra pressure on \uc2 Volkswagen\uc2 's management to reach an agreement that included cost cuts.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020241221ekcl0004j\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
