\par\par \uc2 Foreign Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Can Wall Street Pressure Ease Brutality in India?\b0\par\par\uc2 By Megha Rajagopalan \par \uc2 1,133 \uc2 words\par \uc2 21 December 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 6\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Pension funds and big investors are pressuring \uc2 Coca-Cola\uc2 , \uc2 Pepsico\uc2  and others over brutal working conditions in India's cane fields. Some of the sugar buyers are tiptoeing toward change.\par \par \uc2 The New York City comptroller, who oversees hundreds of billions of dollars in pension investments, is pressuring some of the world's major sugar buyers to stop profiting off child labor, debt bondage and coerced hysterectomies in western India.\par \par \uc2 The city's pension funds own nearly $1 billion in stock in \uc2 Coca-Cola\uc2 , \uc2 Pepsico\uc2 , \uc2 Mondelez\uc2  and others. Those companies, or their franchisees, are among those that buy sugar from the Indian state of Maharashtra. An investigation by \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  and The Fuller Project this year revealed a brutal, endemic labor system there -- one that is at times enforced by threat of kidnapping and assault.\par \par \uc2 ''We will bring pressure to bear on the companies we invest in who participate in that system by sourcing their supply from it and by funding it,'' the New York comptroller, Brad Lander, said in a recent interview.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Lander is urging companies to work with labor groups in Maharashtra and to demand supply-chain improvements. He has rallied institutional investors including \uc2 BNP Paribas Asset Management\uc2 , based in Paris; Sands Capital of Virginia; and the London-based \uc2 Schroders\uc2  to do the same. Those firms hold hundreds of millions of dollars in stock in the sugar-buying companies.\par \par \uc2 The Biden administration is also applying pressure. The State Department has encouraged American companies to use their buying power as leverage to push sugar mills to make changes, according to a State Department official who spoke on condition of anonymity to discuss private conversations. Diplomats have also encouraged companies to work with labor unions.\par \par \uc2 An ethics body that advises Norway's sovereign wealth fund (the world's largest, at about $1.8 trillion) is also investigating at least one company in its portfolio, according to people with direct knowledge of the inquiry. The company under investigation is unclear. The fund holds stock in two sugar mill owners, \uc2 Dalmia Bharat\uc2  and \uc2 DCM Shriram\uc2 , and in a major \uc2 Pepsico\uc2  franchisee, \uc2 Varun Beverages\uc2 , according to public disclosures.\par \par \uc2 The U.A.W. Retiree Medical Benefits Trust, a $60.5 billion pension fund, signed a letter pushing companies to improve labor practices, according to people with knowledge of the matter.\par \par \uc2 Some of those who confirmed the efforts spoke on condition of anonymity because they were not authorized to speak to reporters or did not want to undermine their outreach to the companies. The banks and investment firms themselves declined to comment.\par \par \uc2 The moves represent an attempt to pressure companies that, otherwise, face little incentive to change. Sugar is a huge industry in Maharashtra, so buyers and producers alike have tremendous amounts at stake. Indian politicians could pass and enforce labor laws, but they own most sugar mills in Maharashtra and deny or downplay problems.\par \par \uc2 Abuse is well documented. Female workers are pushed to get hysterectomies to keep working, often to avoid menstruating or becoming pregnant in dangerous, unsanitary conditions. The industry is also rife with child labor. Workers are kept in debt bondage, The Times and The Fuller Project have reported.\par \par \uc2 The revelations have become a diplomatic issue, too. A group representing cooperative sugar mills in Maharashtra said that it had complained to the U.S. Consulate in the Indian city of Mumbai about ''misleading'' news reports. The group suggested that there was a conspiracy to discourage companies from buying Maharashtra sugar.\par \par \uc2 None of the investors are calling for that. Many say they would prefer that corporations keep buying from Maharashtra and pushing for improved labor practices.\par \par \uc2 But a core issue is that Maharashtra sugar mills pay their laborers through middlemen and reject responsibility for working conditions, minimum wages and other basic rights. Laborers are paid advances that function as loans, often without documentation.\par \par \uc2 No companies appear to be seeking fundamental change in how workers are recruited and paid. Labor experts say that, until they do, exploitation will continue.\par \par \uc2 ''We would be encouraging companies to provide remedy to workers,'' said Caroline Boden of Mercy Investment Services, a small asset manager that focuses on socially responsible investing.\par \par \uc2 There are tentative signs that companies are responding. Here is how some have reacted.\par \par \uc2 Coca-Cola\par \par \uc2 The world's largest beverage company has known since at least 2019 that children were cutting sugar for them in Maharashtra and that laborers were working off debts to their employers.\par \par \uc2 The company said this month that a group it had helped establish would, in January, begin providing at least some cane cutters with first aid, health and safety training. Four mills, including three that Coke buys from, will participate, the company said. The group will also train factory middlemen on wage transparency and seek to formalize contracts with farm workers.\par \par \uc2 The company also said that it had worked to provide clean water, sanitation and female hygiene products to fieldworkers.\par \par \uc2 Coke would not say how much money it had invested in those efforts.\par \par \uc2 Pepsico\par \par \uc2 Pepsi said this spring that it was looking into the reports of abuses.\par \par \uc2 Varun Beverages\uc2 , a major Pepsi franchisees, this year opened its third manufacturing and bottling plant in Maharashtra.\par \par \uc2 Pepsi has said that its franchisee buys relatively little sugar from Maharashtra when taken in the context of the state's huge production.\par \par \uc2 Nestl\u233\'00\'E9\par \par \uc2 At first, \uc2 Nestl\u233\'00\'E9\uc2  told The Times that it had stopped buying sugar from Maharashtra last year ''based on the due diligence work we conduct as part of our human rights framework.''\par \par \uc2 That suggested that the company had uncovered abuses.\par \par \uc2 Pressed for details, a spokeswoman said that she had been mistaken. She said that \uc2 Nestl\u233\'00\'E9\uc2  had stopped buying from Maharashtra because of a 2022 Indian ban on sugar exports.\par \par \uc2 A company document dated late last year, however, lists Maharashtra suppliers. \uc2 Nestl\u233\'00\'E9\uc2  said that document was outdated.\par \par \uc2 In interviews, one sugar mill said that it had sold to \uc2 Nestl\u233\'00\'E9\uc2  this year. Others said that \uc2 Nestl\u233\'00\'E9\uc2  continued to buy from them through brokers.\par \par \uc2 Nestl\u233\'00\'E9\uc2  says that is inaccurate.\par \par \uc2 Mondelez\par \par \uc2 Mondelez\uc2 , a conglomerate that includes Cadbury and Nabisco, said that it was ''taking steps to help address labor concerns on sugar farms in this region.'' It did not elaborate.\par \par \uc2 The company said it had stopped buying sugar from the Indian sugar company \uc2 Dalmia Bharat\uc2 , which owns a mill linked to unnecessary hysterectomies and abuses.\par \par \uc2 Dalmia, which also supplies \uc2 Coca-Cola\uc2 , recently arranged to provide sanitary pads, shoes, socks and gloves to some workers. A doctor had spoken to female workers about menstrual health.\par \par \uc2 Qadri Inzamam, a reporter with The Fuller Project, and Ankur Tangade contributed reporting from Beed, India.\par \par \uc2 Qadri Inzamam, a reporter with The Fuller Project, and Ankur Tangade contributed reporting from Beed, India.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020241221ekcl00044\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
