\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 Amazon\uc2  Reaches Settlement to Resolve Federal Worker Safety Accusations\b0\par\par\uc2 By Noam Scheiber \par \uc2 715 \uc2 words\par \uc2 21 December 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 4\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The Occupational Safety and Health Administration\uc2  agreed to withdraw citations at nine of 10 warehouses where it had flagged violations; the company agreed to a $145,000 penalty.\par \par \uc2 Federal health and safety regulators announced a corporatewide settlement with \uc2 Amazon\uc2  on Thursday, bringing an end to cases involving accusations of safety violations at 10 warehouses in several states.\par \par \uc2 The Occupational Safety and Health Administration\uc2  had cited \uc2 Amazon\uc2  for exposing workers to elevated risks of joint and soft-tissue injuries. The agency had said the injury risks were tied to the pace at which workers lifted items, the body movements required to lift the items and the weight of the items.\par \par \uc2 Under the settlement, \uc2 OSHA\uc2  agreed to withdraw citations at nine of the 10 warehouses. At the 10th warehouse, which handles bulky products like televisions and furniture in Illinois, \uc2 Amazon\uc2  agreed to pay a fine of $145,000, up from the $15,625 the agency initially proposed.\par \par \uc2 The agency said the settlement committed \uc2 Amazon\uc2  to regularly assessing the injury risk faced by employees nationwide, and to putting in place pilot programs to address these risks as needed.\par \par \uc2 ''This settlement requires \uc2 Amazon\uc2  to take action at the corporate level to ensure corporatewide ergonomic requirements are effectively implemented at its warehouses,'' said Seema Nanda, the Labor Department's solicitor.\par \par \uc2 Amazon\uc2  said the settlement, in effect, directed the company to continue relying on its existing safety programs and to ensure that the programs were carried out across the entire company.\par \par \uc2 It said the only specific changes mandated by the settlement were at the warehouse that handles bulky items, where \uc2 Amazon\uc2  will have to designate an official to oversee safety issues stemming from how workers move on the job. The settlement does not appear to mandate any specific physical changes to warehouse equipment or layout.\par \par \uc2 ''We appreciate \uc2 OSHA\uc2 's willingness to consider all the facts and reach today's agreement with us, and we look forward to continuing to work with them going forward,'' \uc2 Amazon\uc2  said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 The company said it had made considerable progress in reducing injury rates since 2019, partly as a result of more than $1 billion it had spent on worker safety during that time. \uc2 Amazon\uc2  has said its injury rates last year were below the industry average reflected in the most recent federal data.\par \par \uc2 Unions and other advocates for workers have long accused \uc2 Amazon\uc2  of setting production targets that require employees to work at speeds that create a high risk of injury, and of using methods for benchmarking injury rates that are misleading.\par \par \uc2 A report released this week by the majority staff of the Senate Health, Education, Labor and Pensions Committee found that the company's injury rates had been roughly double those of the rest of the industry for the last seven years. That report compared \uc2 Amazon\uc2 's injury rates with those of all other warehouses, rather than with an industry average that includes \uc2 Amazon\uc2 's data in the overall set and includes only large warehouses.\par \par \uc2 The committee staff report also unearthed two internal \uc2 Amazon\uc2  studies concluding that the speed at which employees lifted and moved items, typically in response to production quotas, most likely led to higher injury rates.\par \par \uc2 Amazon\uc2  executives rejected the internal studies' recommendations that the company take steps to ease the pace of work at its warehouses, according to documents obtained by the Senate staff. The committee staff also found that \uc2 Amazon\uc2  executives worried that taking such steps could hurt productivity. The company later generated evidence that some of the proposed changes wouldn't reduce injury rates.\par \par \uc2 Amazon\uc2  has long denied that it has strict production quotas and said the Senate committee report was based on ''out-of-date documents and unverifiable anecdotes.''\par \par \uc2 Thursday's settlement does not affect an investigation by the office of the U.S. attorney for the Southern District of New York, which is examining whether \uc2 Amazon\uc2  engaged in a ''fraudulent scheme designed to hide the true number of injuries to \uc2 Amazon\uc2  workers,'' and whether the company misreported injury rates while trying to obtain loans.\par \par \uc2 Maureen Lynch Vogel, an \uc2 Amazon\uc2  spokeswoman, said, ''We believe D.O.J.'s legal theory lacks merit, and we're cooperating fully with their investigation.''\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020241221ekcl00038\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
