\par\par \uc2 Foreign Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Automakers Struggle With Market Shift and Competition From China\b0\par\par\uc2 By Jack Ewing \par \uc2 1,530 \uc2 words\par \uc2 15 December 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 10\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Changing technology, political turmoil and competition from China are cutting into profits and forcing carmakers to cut jobs and close factories.\par \par \uc2 A few years ago, automakers were celebrating record profits as the pandemic created shortages of new cars, allowing them to raise prices. Now the hangover is setting in.\par \par \uc2 Nissan\uc2 , the Japanese automaker, is laying off 9,000 employees. \uc2 Volkswagen\uc2  is considering closing factories in Germany for the first time. The chief executive of the U.S. and European automaker \uc2 Stellantis\uc2 , which owns \uc2 Jeep\uc2 , \uc2 Peugeot\uc2 , \uc2 Fiat\uc2  and other brands, quit after sales tumbled. Even luxury brands like \uc2 BMW\uc2  and \uc2 Mercedes-Benz\uc2  are struggling.\par \par \uc2 Each carmaker has its own problems, but there are some common threads. They include a tricky and expensive technological transition, political turmoil, rising protectionism and the emergence of a new class of fast-growing Chinese carmakers. The many woes raise questions about the future of companies that are a critical source of jobs in many Western and Asian countries.\par \par \uc2 Many of these problems have been apparent for years but became less pressing during the pandemic, lulling some automakers into complacency. When shortages of semiconductors and other components slowed production and limited inventory, carmakers found it easy to raise prices.\par \par \uc2 But that era is over and the industry has reverted to its prepandemic state, with too many carmakers chasing too few buyers.\par \par \uc2 Many car factories around the world are making many fewer cars than they were built to produce. When automakers don't earn a decent return on their factories and machines, there is ''a massive effect on profitability,'' said Simon Croom, a professor of supply chain management at the \uc2 University of San Diego\uc2 . ''The difference between profit and loss is a very fine line in the auto industry.''\par \par \uc2 Workers are among the first to suffer in an industry that employs nine million people worldwide in manufacturing, including around a million in the United States. More than two million more Americans work for dealers and related businesses.\par \par \uc2 Nissan\uc2 , which has factories in Mississippi and Tennessee, has not detailed where its layoffs will take place. It is not alone in cutting jobs. Ford last month announced 4,000 job cuts, mostly at factories in Britain and Germany. The company cited ''unprecedented competitive, regulatory, and economic headwinds.''\par \par \uc2 Ford was partly referring to Chinese carmakers. Barely a factor before the pandemic, they have charged into the international market with cars that can match Japanese, European or American vehicles on quality, at much lower prices.\par \par \uc2 BYD, Chery, SAIC and other Chinese carmakers are still effectively barred from the United States by trade rules and hobbled by tariffs in Europe. But they are pushing into places like Australia, Brazil, Chile and Thailand, luring buyers away from the likes of \uc2 Fiat\uc2 , \uc2 General Motors\uc2  and \uc2 Toyota\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Competition from China is ''starting to hit the safe places that Western carmakers had,'' said Felipe Munoz, global analyst at \uc2 JATO Dynamics\uc2 , a research firm.\par \par \uc2 Inside China, the world's largest car market, domestic manufacturers have stolen the limelight from foreign carmakers with over-the-top options like those offered by BYD in its Yangwang U8, a plug-in hybrid off-road vehicle.\par \par \uc2 The U8 can stay afloat for up to 30 minutes in a flood, according to the company, and its wheels can be set to roll so that the vehicle can rotate 360 degrees while remaining in the same spot.\par \par \uc2 The rise of Chinese automakers has been particularly tough on German carmakers. \uc2 Volkswagen\uc2  gets one-third of its sales from China and once dominated that market. But the company's deliveries there plunged 10 percent in the first nine months of this year, compared with 2023.\par \par \uc2 BMW\uc2  and \uc2 Mercedes-Benz\uc2  have also reported big sales declines in China recently, which they blamed for lower profits.\par \par \uc2 American carmakers have also suffered from this shift. G.M. said this month that it would take a more than $5 billion hit to its profit as it restructured its Chinese operations, which have been losing money.\par \par \uc2 Companies that were slow to replace aging models are doing worst. That has been the case for \uc2 Nissan\uc2 , \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  and even \uc2 Tesla\uc2 , which analysts expect to end the year with sales that are roughly unchanged from 2023. Others have struggled to build appealing electric vehicles and develop software, an increasingly important element of car design.\par \par \uc2 Volkswagen\uc2  was among the first established carmakers to develop electric vehicles, but the models underwhelmed buyers and critics. Sales in the United States of the company's ID.4 sport-utility vehicle plunged by more than half in the third quarter from a year earlier, according to \uc2 Kelley Blue Book\uc2 . Buggy software handicapped sales of the ID.4 and other electric models that \uc2 Volkswagen\uc2  sells in Europe and Asia.\par \par \uc2 ''The Chinese are winning market share and the Germans are losing,'' said \uc2 Ferdinand Dudenh\u246\'00\'F6ffer\uc2 , director of the Center for Automotive Research in Bochum, Germany. ''It's not only the electric cars, it's the software in the cars.''\par \par \uc2 Changing government policy is adding to the carmakers' woes. Sales of electric vehicles plunged in Germany after the government, facing a budget crisis, abruptly eliminated financial incentives.\par \par \uc2 In the United States, President-elect Donald J. Trump and Republicans in Congress want to repeal Biden-era tax credits designed to promote electric vehicles. The shifts in policy endanger the hundreds of billions of dollars that G.M., Hyundai-Kia, \uc2 Volkswagen\uc2  and others have invested in new factories and cars.\par \par \uc2 ''The auto industry has had to dish out a lot of capital for an underwhelming E.V. market and one that may well change in the next six months,'' said Erin Keating, an executive analyst at \uc2 Cox Automotive\uc2 , a research firm.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Trump has also threatened to impose tariffs on imports from China, Mexico and Canada. China is an important source of components for virtually all carmakers. Mexico is an important manufacturing center for \uc2 BMW\uc2 , G.M., Ford, \uc2 Stellantis\uc2 , \uc2 Volkswagen\uc2  and others, shipping two million vehicles to the United States in the first nine months of the year.\par \par \uc2 Not all car companies are doing poorly. G.M. shares have risen more than 40 percent this year, while shares of most automakers have fallen. In part, Wall Street is rewarding G.M. for popular electric vehicles like the Cadillac Lyriq and Chevrolet Equinox. \uc2 Mary T. Barra\uc2 , the G.M. chief executive, has said the company is close to making a profit on electric vehicles, unlike other American carmakers excluding \uc2 Tesla\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But G.M. is also retrenching, announcing last week that it would stop developing robotaxis, autonomous vehicles that can carry passengers without drivers. The decision raised questions about whether established carmakers can compete with \uc2 Tesla\uc2  and \uc2 Waymo\uc2 , a division of \uc2 Google\uc2 's parent company, in the next generation of automotive technology.\par \par \uc2 Toyota\uc2 's strategy of doubling down on hybrids, while offering few all-electric cars, also appears to be paying off -- at least for now. Many buyers are choosing hybrids, which cost a lot less than electric vehicles and don't need to be charged, as a way to reduce emissions and fuel expenses.\par \par \uc2 But \uc2 Toyota\uc2  could be left behind if sales of electric vehicles grow faster than market analysts expect. Prices for battery-powered vehicles are dropping while the distance they can travel on a charge is growing. In China, electric vehicles are already cheaper than comparable gasoline models. More than half of new cars sold there are electric or plug-in hybrids.\par \par \uc2 Carmakers are trying to adapt. \uc2 Stellantis\uc2 , whose chief executive, \uc2 Carlos Tavares\uc2 , quit this month, has new models lined up for 2025. They include several electric vehicles, among them Jeeps, Ram pickups and a Dodge Charger muscle car. The company is also working to repair its relationship with dealers who feel that \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  waited too long to lower prices and offer incentives to help them sell cars that were piling up on their lots.\par \par \uc2 John Elkann\uc2 , the chairman of \uc2 Stellantis\uc2 , held a virtual meeting with U.S. dealers after Mr. Tavares's resignation. Later, the Stellantis National Dealer Council issued a statement expressing optimism that ''2025 will mark a turning point.''\par \par \uc2 Attempts to reach Mr. Tavares for comment were unsuccessful.\par \par \uc2 Market pressures will prompt carmakers to cooperate with one another more, for example, by sharing the costs of engine development, said K. Venkatesh Prasad, the senior vice president of research at the Center for Automotive Research in Ann Arbor, Mich.\par \par \uc2 ''You're seeing some of this already,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 Makoto Uchida\uc2 , \uc2 Nissan\uc2 's chief executive, said last month that the company would reduce the time it takes to develop a new vehicle by working more closely with its partners \uc2 Renault\uc2 , \uc2 Mitsubishi\uc2  and \uc2 Honda\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Volkswagen\uc2  is trying to solve its software problems by investing in \uc2 Rivian\uc2 , which makes electric pickups and sport utility vehicles in Illinois.\par \par \uc2 Western carmakers are also making deals with Chinese automakers. \uc2 Volkswagen\uc2  will develop new models with \uc2 Xpeng\uc2  to sell in China. Last year, \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  bought a 20 percent stake in Leapmotor and has begun selling the Chinese company's electric vehicles in Europe.\par \par \uc2 ''If you can't beat 'em,'' Mr. Dudenh\u246\'00\'F6ffer said, ''join 'em.''\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020241215ekcf00066\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
