\par\par \uc2 Metropolitan Desk; SECTMB\par \b \uc2 City Aims to Prevent Driver Lockouts by \uc2 Uber\uc2  and \uc2 Lyft\b0\par\par\uc2 By Lola Fadulu \par \uc2 1,810 \uc2 words\par \uc2 15 December 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 As ride-hailing companies and labor unions each push New York City to amend its rules, drivers hurt by widespread app lockouts are struggling to make ends meet.\par \par \uc2 New York City was the first place in the United States to mandate minimum pay rates for drivers for companies like \uc2 Uber\uc2  and \uc2 Lyft\uc2 . But after the companies found a loophole that made it much harder for some drivers to get by, the city is looking to change its law, prompting dueling pressure campaigns as both sides seek to influence the outcome.\par \par \uc2 Earlier this year, the two companies began imposing what came to be known as lockouts, which prevented drivers from signing into the applications during certain periods. The companies said the city's minimum pay law, which takes into account how much of drivers' working time is spent transporting passengers, had forced their hands. \uc2 Bloomberg\uc2  first reported on the lockouts.\par \par \uc2 Amadou Diallo, 38, is one of many drivers who say the lockouts have severely affected his take-home pay. Mr. Diallo, who has been driving full-time for \uc2 Uber\uc2  and \uc2 Lyft\uc2  for four years, said he used to make up to $350 on a given weekend day, before expenses, but is now making less than $200 during such shifts because of the lockouts.\par \par \uc2 Uber\uc2  stopped locking out drivers in September, two months after reaching a temporary agreement with the city, but has since begun a push to lower drivers' pay. \uc2 Lyft\uc2  has continued the lockouts, and Mr. Diallo has felt the financial impact. An entire week can go by without the ability to sign into \uc2 Lyft\uc2  in New York, he said, and he has had to travel to New Jersey just to get access.\par \par \uc2 ''I have to reduce so many things,'' Mr. Diallo said, adding that he can no longer afford to live alone and had to move in with roommates in Flatbush, Brooklyn.\par \par \uc2 He has also stopped going out to restaurants with his family on the weekends and buying new clothes.\par \par \uc2 ''Everything is going tight,'' Mr. Diallo said.\par \par \uc2 The Taxi and Limousine Commission, the city agency that oversees the ride-share companies, plans to amend the driver pay law early next year to prevent the companies from engaging in lockouts. As the city mulls over how it will adjust the law, unions that represent drivers have made their demands clear, and so have \uc2 Uber\uc2  and \uc2 Lyft\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The Independent Drivers Guild, which represents around 80,000 for-hire drivers in New York City, demanded this week that the city increase the amount of available work by limiting the pool of new \uc2 Uber\uc2  and \uc2 Lyft\uc2  drivers, which the union believes would end the lockouts.\par \par \uc2 The union also called on the city to raise driver pay to account for the rising cost of living. It estimates that thousands of drivers are on the brink of homelessness because of the lockouts.\par \par \uc2 Uber\uc2  has argued that the city's drivers are compensated fairly and that their pay should in fact be lowered. The company released a study last month that found that after driving-related expenses, the average driver makes more than double the city's minimum wage.\par \par \uc2 The company has also pointed to the city's own data, which shows that overall pay for full-time drivers was up in every month from May to October this year compared with last year. The city increased per-trip pay by 3.49 percent in February to account for rising expenses.\par \par \uc2 Uber\uc2  has petitioned the city to reduce pay rates because certain expenses, such as gas and car prices, had dropped. It warned that if the city did not agree, the company would significantly reduce its work force.\par \par \uc2 Lyft\uc2  has not gone as far as \uc2 Uber\uc2 , and has said it would like to see the city keep the minimum pay rates, but remove a requirement that drivers be busy a certain amount of the time, which is known as the utilization rate. That change would bring the law more in line with the pay models in California, Washington and Massachusetts.\par \par \uc2 Protests have gone on for months as the city weighs what to do next. Most recently, on Wednesday, the Independent Drivers Guild organized a caravan of drivers to demonstrate outside the Taxi and Limousine Commission office in Queens.\par \par \uc2 Over the summer, in a protest led by the New York Taxi Workers Alliance, nearly 3,000 drivers marched from City Hall to \uc2 Uber\uc2 's headquarters. Drivers also formed a protest motorcade that stretched nearly 20 blocks, taking up three lanes of traffic.\par \par \uc2 ''Without the drivers, there is no \uc2 Uber\uc2 , there is no \uc2 Lyft\uc2 ,'' said David Williams, 55, who has taken part in several demonstrations, including Wednesday's.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Williams has been driving for both companies since November 2014. He said his weekly income before expenses was $1,800 to $2,500 before the lockouts began, and is now around $1,600. He can no longer afford his mortgage payments, he said.\par \par \uc2 He finds it insulting that \uc2 Uber\uc2  is seeking to lower drivers' pay and has claimed that they are, on average, well compensated.\par \par \uc2 ''Nothing can be further from the truth,'' Mr. Williams said.\par \par \uc2 The city created its driver pay law, which went into effect in 2019, after finding that 85 percent of drivers for ride-hailing apps were making less than minimum wage.\par \par \uc2 The law established a minimum amount that drivers could expect to earn from each ride, based on a formula that involved the distance of the trip, the time it took and the percentage of overall driving time spent with passengers.\par \par \uc2 The last factor is known as the utilization rate. The formula for 2024 dictates that drivers need to have a passenger more than 53 percent of the time they are working. If, at the end of the year, the city finds that drivers are not meeting that benchmark, the utilization rate for the following year will be lowered accordingly. The lower the utilization rate, the higher the minimum amounts the companies are required to pay drivers.\par \par \uc2 Including the utilization rate in the pay structure incentivizes the companies to manage their driver pools well, said Jason Kersten, a spokesman for the Taxi and Limousine Comission, said of the companies.\par \par \uc2 Laura Padin, who specializes in workplace standards at the National Employment Law Project, agreed, saying the formula was designed to reduce drivers' downtime. The goal is to make it so ''workers don't have a lot of time waiting, which can really eat up their income,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 The lockouts began in late May, when \uc2 Uber\uc2  and \uc2 Lyft\uc2  began displaying messages to drivers saying they could not use the apps because of the city's regulations around pay.\par \par \uc2 Lockouts ensured there would be fewer drivers on the road, causing the utilization rate to appear higher, which meant the companies would not be required to pay drivers more in 2025.\par \par \uc2 Josh Gold, a spokesman for \uc2 Uber\uc2 , said the company had limited drivers' access to its app ''during times of low demand'' this summer.\par \par \uc2 ''New York City drivers took home more money, spent more time online and did more trips on average while restricted access took place, compared to the same time period a year ago,'' Mr. Gold said.\par \par \uc2 Lyft\uc2  said it started lockouts only after \uc2 Uber\uc2  did, adding that it had tried to improve its utilization rate for months before that.\par \par \uc2 CJ Macklin, a spokesman for \uc2 Lyft\uc2 , said the lockouts were ''unfortunately the direct result of the city's complex driver pay system.'' He added that a consequence was that ''drivers face reduced earning opportunities and riders experience longer wait times.''\par \par \uc2 He said if drivers were paid more, riders in New York, who already pay some of the highest fares in the country, would ultimately bear the cost.\par \par \uc2 Michael Reich, a professor of economics at the \uc2 University of California, Berkeley\uc2 , who helped the city design its pay structure, called the lockouts an effort to ''game the formula,'' adding that the practice ''takes away any kind of predictability for earnings, particularly for those drivers who are working more than full time.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Reich said the lockouts also removed the flexibility that attracted drivers to the platform in the first place. ''The drivers have been sold on the idea that they can turn on the app whenever they want to,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 And Mr. Kersten from the Taxi and Limousine Commission dismissed the companies' explanations, noting that the city's rules did not require them to lock people out. ''It's a choice they are making,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 That choice seems to have changed the lives of thousands of drivers in the city.\par \par \uc2 In a survey of more than 5,000 drivers conducted by the Independent Drivers Guild, 67 percent of respondents reported having lost income of $5,000 or more from late May to early December, and nearly 90 percent said they had struggled to pay their bills because of the lockouts.\par \par \uc2 Brendan Sexton, the union's president, said the temporary agreement the city reached with \uc2 Uber\uc2  and \uc2 Lyft\uc2  in July to phase out lockouts was a step in the right direction.\par \par \uc2 But, he added, ''there needs to be a long-term solution to make sure that drivers get the right pay, that they're treated with dignity.''\par \par \uc2 Ibrahim Zoure, 61, started driving for the apps in 2017 after quitting his job with the city's Access-A-Ride paratransit service, where he made $12 an hour as a driver.\par \par \uc2 He said when he joined \uc2 Uber\uc2  in 2017, he made between $2,500 and $3,000 a week, working 12- to 15-hour days. But as more drivers joined the apps, his pay started to go down. And when the lockouts began, he said, it declined sharply. In October, for instance, Mr. Zoure said he made around $700 on average each week.\par \par \uc2 ''I feel like I've been deceived by \uc2 Uber\uc2  and \uc2 Lyft\uc2 ,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 He said it has been difficult to find other work to supplement his income because of his age. He has been unable to pay his $2,000 rent in the Brownsville area of Brooklyn and said his landlord planned to take him to court for eviction proceedings in February.\par \par \uc2 ''What they are doing is just creating poor people,'' he said of the companies. ''My credit is totally ruined. My bank account is empty. I have no money and I'm getting a collection call every day.''\par \par \uc2 Frank Haley, 46, who has driven for \uc2 Uber\uc2  and \uc2 Lyft\uc2  since 2016, said he had not been able to get rides through \uc2 Lyft\uc2  for the last couple of months. He has depleted much of his savings.\par \par \uc2 ''There is no income; there's no money to be made,'' Mr. Haley said. ''This is going to force a lot of drivers out of the industry.''\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020241215ekcf0004q\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
