\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 Child Labor Investigated At Subsidiary Of \uc2 HelloFresh\b0\par\par\uc2 By Johnny Diaz \par \uc2 603 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 December 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The Labor Department investigation focuses on a plant in Aurora, Ill., operated by Factor75, a meal kit delivery company that \uc2 HelloFresh\uc2  acquired in 2020.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 U.S. Labor Department\uc2  is investigating allegations that migrant children have worked at a meal preparation delivery facility in Illinois that is a subsidiary of the popular meal kit company \uc2 HelloFresh\uc2 , the company and federal regulators said.\par \par \uc2 At least six teenagers, including some from Guatemala, worked night shifts at the facility, Cristobal Cavazos, the executive director for Immigrant Solidarity, an immigrant rights advocacy group that helped report the claims to federal regulators, told ABC News, which reported on the investigation.\par \par \uc2 ''They're minors working dangerous jobs,'' Mr. Cavazos told ABC News, which reported that the children were working there as recently as last summer.\par \par \uc2 In a statement on Monday, a spokesman for the Labor Department confirmed an investigation into \uc2 HelloFresh\uc2  and a temporary staffing agency, Midway Staffing. The spokesman declined to comment further, citing the open investigation.\par \par \uc2 It was not clear what kind of jobs the minors had at the facility.\par \par \uc2 The claims relate to a Factor75 facility in Aurora, Ill., according to a \uc2 HelloFresh\uc2  spokeswoman who said the company learned of the claims from the Labor Department. In 2020, \uc2 HelloFresh\uc2  acquired Factor75, which provides ready-to-eat meals to customers.\par \par \uc2 In a statement on Sunday, the \uc2 HelloFresh\uc2  spokeswoman said: ''We were deeply troubled to learn of the allegations made against a former temporary staffing agency, Midway Staffing. As soon as we learned of these allegations, we immediately terminated the relationship.''\par \par \uc2 ''We have zero tolerance for any form of child labor,'' she added, ''and we have taken action to ensure no minors perform work in or have access to our facilities.''\par \par \uc2 Midway Staffing said in a statement on Monday that it was cooperating with the Labor Department's investigation.\par \par \uc2 ''Midway Staffing denies that its actions or practices contributed to or caused any alleged child labor violations,'' R.J. Parrilli, the company's chief executive, said in the statement.\par \par \uc2 He added, ''Midway Staffing refuses to knowingly hire anyone who is not at least 18 years of age.''\par \par \uc2 Founded in Berlin in 2011, \uc2 HelloFresh\uc2  delivers meal kits with ingredients measured out for specific recipes. The company, which operates in more than a dozen countries, was one of the earliest entrants in an industry that includes other big players such as \uc2 Blue Apron\uc2  and Plated.\par \par \uc2 The Labor Department said that employers nationwide were fined more than $15.1 million for child labor violations in the fiscal year that ended on Sept. 30, an 89 percent increase from the previous year. Those penalties were related to 736 investigations that uncovered violations involving more than 4,000 children.\par \par \uc2 Under federal law, minors are prohibited from working in dangerous jobs that are common in the meat processing industry, where there is a high risk of injury.\par \par \uc2 The investigation is the latest by the department to target companies that employ children. Last month, the department said it had fined a cleaning company in Oklahoma nearly $172,000 after an investigation found that it had hired nearly a dozen children to work dangerous overnight shifts at a slaughterhouse in Sioux City, Iowa, operated by Seaboard Triumph Foods.\par \par \uc2 In May, a cleaning company in Tennessee was ordered to pay more than $649,000 in civil penalties after federal regulators found that it had employed at least two dozen children as young as 13 to work overnight shifts at the Sioux City structure and at a \uc2 Perdue Farms\uc2  plant on the eastern shore of Virginia.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020241214ekce0002y\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
