\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 Wall Street Edges Back From Records Ahead of Jobs Report\b0\par\par\uc2 By The Associated Press \par \uc2 690 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 December 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The huge rally for stocks lost momentum on Thursday as Wall Street counted down to a big jobs report on Friday. The crypto market had more action, and Bitcoin briefly burst to a record above $103,000 before pulling back.\par \par \uc2 The S&P 500 slipped 0.2 percent from the record high it had set the day before, its 56th of the year so far, to shave a bit off what is set to be one of its best years of the millennium. The Dow Jones industrial average fell 0.6 percent, while the Nasdaq composite slipped 0.2 percent from its own record set the day before.\par \par \uc2 Bitcoin powered above $100,000 for the first time the night before, after President-elect Donald J. Trump chose \uc2 Paul Atkins\uc2 , who is seen as a crypto advocate, as his nominee to head the \uc2 Securities and Exchange Commission\uc2 . The cryptocurrency fell back toward $99,000 on Thursday, according to CoinDesk.\par \par \uc2 Sharp swings for Bitcoin are nothing new, and they took stocks of companies enmeshed in the crypto world on a similar ride. After rising as much as 9 percent in early trading, \uc2 MicroStrategy\uc2 , a company that has been raising cash just to buy Bitcoin, swung to a loss of 4.8 percent. Shares of the crypto exchange \uc2 Coinbase Global\uc2  fell 3.1 percent after likewise erasing a big early gain.\par \par \uc2 Elsewhere on Wall Street, stocks of airlines helped lead the way following the latest increases in financial forecasts from carriers.\par \par \uc2 American Airlines Group\uc2  soared 16.8 percent after saying it was making more in revenue during the last three months of 2024 than it expected, and it will likely make a bigger profit than it had earlier forecast. The airline also chose \uc2 Citi\uc2  to be its exclusive partner for credit cards that give miles in its loyalty program. That should help its cash coming in from co-branded credit card and other partners grow by about 10 percent annually.\par \par \uc2 Southwest Airlines\uc2  climbed 2 percent after saying it was seeing stronger demand from leisure travelers than it expected. It also raised its forecast for revenue for the holiday traveling season.\par \par \uc2 On the losing end of Wall Street was Synposys, which tumbled 12.4 percent. The supplier for the semiconductor industry reported better profit for the latest quarter than analysts expected, but it also warned of ''continued macro uncertainties'' and gave a forecast for revenue in the current quarter that fell short of some analysts' estimates.\par \par \uc2 American Eagle Outfitters\uc2  fell even more, 14.3 percent after the retailer said it was preparing for ''potential choppiness'' outside of peak selling periods.\par \par \uc2 It was reminiscent of a warning from \uc2 Foot Locker\uc2  earlier in the week and raised more concerns about how resilient shoppers can remain.\par \par \uc2 Solid spending by consumers has been one of the main reasons the American economy has avoided a recession that earlier seemed inevitable after the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  raised interest rates to crush inflation. But shoppers are now contending with still-high prices and a slowing job market.\par \par \uc2 This week's highlight for Wall Street will be Friday's jobs report from the federal government, which will show how many people employers hired and fired last month. A report on Thursday said the number of workers applying for unemployment benefits rose last week but remained at historically healthy levels.\par \par \uc2 Expectations are high that the Fed will cut its main interest rate again when it meets in two weeks.\par \par \uc2 The Fed began easing its main interest rate from a two-decade high in September, hoping to offer more support for the job market.\par \par \uc2 In the bond market, the yield on the 10-year Treasury edged down to 4.18 percent from 4.19 percent late Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 In stock markets abroad, indexes were mostly calm in Europe after far-right and left-wing lawmakers in France joined together to vote on a no-confidence motion that will force Prime Minister \uc2 Michel Barnier\uc2  and his Cabinet to resign. The CAC 40 index in Paris added 0.4 percent.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020241206ekc60004n\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
