\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 VW Workers in Germany, Under Threat of Closings, Stage Strikes\b0\par\par\uc2 By Melissa Eddy \par \uc2 654 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 December 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 5\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Workers at nine of the automaker's German factories walked off their jobs for several hours, and warned they would escalate the action if their demands went unmet.\par \par \uc2 Volkswagen\uc2  workers across Germany escalated their labor dispute with management by walking off their jobs for several hours on Monday, and their union threatened longer strikes if their demands were not met.\par \par \uc2 The automaker is in the middle of labor negotiations with \uc2 IG Metall\uc2 , the union representing most of its workers, as the company tries to reduce costs in an effort to return it to profitability. \uc2 Volkswagen\uc2  is seeking 10 percent wage cuts and threatening to close factories in Germany, the first such move in its 87-year history.\par \par \uc2 Thousands of workers at nine of the company's plants in Germany, as well as several other subsidiaries that are covered under a wage agreement with the automaker, staged two-hour strikes on Monday, demanding that \uc2 Volkswagen\uc2  guarantee their jobs and keep its factories open.\par \par \uc2 IG Metall\uc2  has threatened to start longer walkouts, or open-ended strikes, unless it is able to reach an agreement with \uc2 Volkswagen\uc2  managers.\par \par \uc2 ''If necessary, this will be the toughest collective-bargaining battle \uc2 Volkswagen\uc2  has ever seen,'' said Thorsten Gr\u246\'00\'F6ger, the chief negotiator and district manager for the union. ''\uc2 Volkswagen\uc2  will have to decide at the negotiating table how long and how intense this dispute has to be.''\par \par \uc2 The labor battle, the company's first involving strikes since 2018, comes as \uc2 Volkswagen\uc2 , Germany's leading automaker, faces slowing demand for its cars in Europe and Asia, as well as increased competition from Chinese automakers.\par \par \uc2 ''\uc2 Volkswagen\uc2  respects the right of employees to take part in a warning strike,'' a company representative said in an emailed statement on Monday, adding that measures had been taken to try to minimize the effect of the strikes on customers.\par \par \uc2 Volkswagen\uc2  remains interested in reaching ''a sustainable and jointly supported solution,'' the company said.\par \par \uc2 The two sides are scheduled to meet next Monday for a fourth round of talks that have so far remained deadlocked. Germany's labor system guarantees employees certain rights in the workplace, and in the case of \uc2 Volkswagen\uc2 , that has meant that protracted strikes are relatively rare.\par \par \uc2 But this fight, coming at a time of political uncertainty after the collapse of the coalition government in Berlin, coupled with a near-stagnant German economy and an automotive industry in transformation, could challenge Germany's traditional, cooperation-based model of labor negotiations.\par \par \uc2 ''It is important that we fight and show our teeth,'' \uc2 Daniela Cavallo\uc2 , who leads \uc2 Volkswagen\uc2 's works council and is a member of \uc2 IG Metall\uc2 , told hundreds of workers who were striking at the company's Wolfsburg headquarters on Monday.\par \par \uc2 Last week, \uc2 Volkswagen\uc2  rejected a union proposal that involved forgoing wage increases in exchange for job guarantees by setting up a fund to be tapped when things get tight in the future. \uc2 IG Metall\uc2  had calculated that the offer would save the company 1.5 billion euros, about $1.6 billion, but \uc2 Volkswagen\uc2  said the proposal fell short of the long-term structural change that it had deemed necessary.\par \par \uc2 Over the weekend, the union hosted a late-night party outside the \uc2 Volkswagen\uc2  factory in Emden to mark the end of a weekslong ''peace period,'' which started at the beginning of wage negotiations but ran out at midnight on Sunday.\par \par \uc2 Bratwursts sizzled on grills, and workers dressed in bright red union vests sipped hot mulled wine, making it look as if they were celebrating the holiday season. But the tone was serious.\par \par \uc2 ''There is no other issue around the dinner table anymore,'' Torsten Hasenpusch, a representative for \uc2 IG Metall\uc2  in Emden, told the German public television broadcaster NDR. ''Will I lose my job? Can we keep up our standard of living? It's all people can think about.''\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020241203ekc30004c\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
