\par\par \uc2 Foreign Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Israel Says Food Aid Worker Whom It Killed Took Part in Oct. 7 Attack\b0\par\par\uc2 By Tim Balk \par \uc2 818 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 December 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 10\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The Israeli military said it killed the worker in a strike in Gaza on Saturday. ''To the best of our knowledge, no \uc2 WCK\uc2  team members are affiliated with \uc2 Hamas\uc2 ,'' a spokeswoman for the aid group said.\par \par \uc2 Israel on Saturday said it had killed a \uc2 World Central Kitchen\uc2  worker it accused of taking part in the \uc2 Hamas\uc2 -led attack that started the war in Gaza last year, in the second Israeli strike to kill workers affiliated with the aid group.\par \par \uc2 A spokeswoman for \uc2 World Central Kitchen\uc2 , a U.S.-based relief group, said on Saturday that three of its contractors had been killed in an Israeli airstrike on a vehicle. In a statement, the aid group said that it ''had no knowledge that any individual in the vehicle had alleged ties'' to the \uc2 Hamas\uc2 -led attack.\par \par \uc2 ''To the best of our knowledge, no \uc2 WCK\uc2  team members are affiliated with \uc2 Hamas\uc2 ,'' the spokeswoman, Linda Roth, said in an email.\par \par \uc2 The Israeli military declined to comment on the two other workers \uc2 World Central Kitchen\uc2  reported were killed.\par \par \uc2 The aid group said it was pausing operations in Gaza, where a dire humanitarian crisis is unfolding for some two million people. The organization took a similar action in April, after seven of its workers were killed in an Israeli airstrike.\par \par \uc2 The Israeli military said on Saturday that the person it targeted in the latest strike had taken part in the Oct. 7 attack on Kibbutz Nir Oz, an Israeli village near the Gaza border where dozens of people were abducted. He had been monitored by Israeli ''intelligence for a while and was struck following credible information regarding his real-time location,'' the military said.\par \par \uc2 It added that it had targeted ''a civilian unmarked vehicle, and its movement on the route was not coordinated for transporting of aid.''\par \par \uc2 Ms. Roth said that the vehicle was not branded, and that the aid group was investigating the situation.\par \par \uc2 COGAT, the Israeli agency responsible for coordinating aid deliveries into Gaza, said it had demanded that \uc2 World Central Kitchen\uc2  investigate its hiring practices after the strike on the vehicle.\par \par \uc2 The aid group said it thoroughly vetted its workers. ''All \uc2 World Central Kitchen\uc2  contractors are queried against the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2 's OFAC list,'' Ms. Roth said, referring to a \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  list of people, institutions and countries considered national security threats. ''Staff employees undergo additional background checks.''\par \par \uc2 The strike and the accusation that followed threatened to reignite tensions between Israeli officials and humanitarian leaders, who have criticized each other over a year of war and worsening conditions in Gaza. Relief groups have accused Israel of allowing too few supplies into the territory and hindering the flow of aid; Israeli officials say they do not restrict the amount of aid that can enter Gaza and argue that aid agencies should be doing more.\par \par \uc2 Aid workers operating in Gaza also face the dangers of the ongoing war and widespread lawlessness in the territory. A large convoy of trucks was ''violently looted'' in Gaza this month, according to the main \uc2 U.N.\uc2  agency that helps Palestinians, known as \uc2 UNRWA\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The Israeli attack on the contractors associated with \uc2 World Central Kitchen\uc2 , a disaster relief organization founded by the celebrity chef Jos\u233\'00\'E9 Andr\u233\'00\'E9s, left the group once again to grapple with the loss of its workers in the Gaza war zone.\par \par \uc2 In early April, the group suspended work in Gaza and across the region after the airstrike that killed seven of its workers.\par \par \uc2 After an investigation, the Israeli military called that attack a ''grave mistake" and cited a series of failures, including a breakdown in communication and violations of the military's operating procedures.\par \par \uc2 The aid group resumed operations a few weeks later with local Palestinian employees, and it remains one of several aid providers that have operated in the territory, which faces a high risk of famine and crises of mass displacement, disease under conditions of wintry weather and continued fighting.\par \par \uc2 In March, \uc2 World Central Kitchen\uc2  became the first group in nearly 20 years to deliver aid to Gaza by sea, briefly using a makeshift jetty fashioned from rubble. The U.S. military also attempted to use a temporary pier to get aid into Gaza, but shut down the project this summer because of rough seas.\par \par \uc2 Israel has also accused a handful of \uc2 UNRWA\uc2  employees of participating in the \uc2 Hamas\uc2 -led Oct. 7 attacks. \uc2 U.N.\uc2  investigators in August cleared 10 employees of the agency, which says it submits its employees' information to Israel, and fired nine other workers because of possible involvement.\par \par \uc2 Israel's ambassador to the United Nations criticized the investigation, and in October the Israeli lawmakers passed two laws that could threaten its work by barring operations in the country.\par \par \uc2 Aaron Boxerman contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Aaron Boxerman contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020241201ekc10006t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
